WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:06.270
- So if I can call the Board to order,

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.090
and let me, first of all,
welcome members of the public

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.350
who've joined us today
to observe the meeting.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.800
We have a number of
apologies today for absence.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.090
So we have apologies from
Mark Walport and David Sloman,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.520
Susan Kilsby, Rakesh Kapoor,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.680
Jeremy Townsend, and Wol Kolade,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.653
and Simon Wessely will be
joining us slightly later.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.950
Can I just ask the Board,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.400
are there any declarations of interest

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.320
that you need to provide today,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.320
in addition to those interests
which are already registered?

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.000
Thank you, and then can I ask
the Board for its approval

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.483
to the minutes of the meeting
held on the 30th of March?

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.783
Thank you. Thank you very much.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.610
Let me just give you a very brief review

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.960
of some of my recent activities,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.517
but also before I do that,

00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:09.360
I'd just like to both
thank and pay tribute

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.150
to one of our colleagues, Laura Wade-Gery,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.930
for whom this actually is the last Board

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.640
that she will attend for NHS England

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.700
before she leaves us at
your last day, I think,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.230
30th of June.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.180
And your leaving us obviously and staying

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.890
within the family going
on to chair Moorfields.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.130
So thank you very much for
that continuing service.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.130
And Laura became the chair of NHS Digital

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.860
in September in 2020.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.620
And, as importantly, actually the review

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.670
that you led into providing
a sort of a coherent approach

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.950
to digital transformation.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.970
That was published I think
in late '21, November 2021.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.760
It was actually called
"Putting Data Digital and Tech

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.100
at the Heart of Transforming the NHS".

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.000
And Laura, you have been
a strong advocate for that

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.580
in many of our private
meetings and engagements

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.460
that we've had, as well
as actually in your role

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.510
on chairing the Board of NHS Digital.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:11.190
You previously, I think you had joined

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.210
the Board of NHS Improvement,
I think in, as a NED in 2018.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.470
So Laura, can I just express
on behalf of the Board

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.130
and the executives all thanks to you

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.510
for all the support
- Yeah.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.900
- and the work and being
such a great colleague.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.678
Thank you very much.

00:02:29.678 --> 00:02:31.290
- Going to the extreme family.
- Yeah.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.870
- Indeed.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.790
Just going on, we are out
with a recruitment round

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.990
for new non-executive directors
to join NHS England Board.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.690
We've had a very strong
list of candidates,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.590
a strong set of applications
over 80 applicants,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.820
and we are in the process
almost finalised actually,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.660
of shortlisting down to interview stage,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.843
and the interviews to be held in mid-June.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.430
Just looking forward to
July, the next Board,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.583
as this Board knows, is
going to be in Birmingham.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.690
We'll be holding normal
Board meetings there,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.700
but we'll also have a
dinner for the Board,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.283
inviting members of the ICB to join us,

00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:19.830
and also Dale Bywater,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.017
the regional director.

00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:24.060
And really importantly, I think,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.080
for those Board members who can do it,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.870
on the Friday after the Board meeting,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.330
I would like as many of us as possible

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.020
separated out probably into
pairs of threes, or so on,

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.010
to go out and visit various
parts of the service

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.510
around the system.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.490
And we're currently
working on the options,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.413
in terms of where we should go.

00:03:45.413 --> 00:03:49.260
It'd be very good to get
intelligence about what's going on

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.093
from the frontline,

00:03:50.093 --> 00:03:51.630
but also actually clearly to represent

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.253
the Board of NHS England
into Birmingham and Solihull.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.840
So we'll come back to you with proposals

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.033
in the next couple of weeks or so.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.010
It's been a busy time,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.740
actually the last few
weeks and even months.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.450
I'd just like to reflect a little bit

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:14.450
and thank the executive team
for all the immensely hard work

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.950
that's going on.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.320
And in particular evident
from a number of the plans

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.930
that have been published recently.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.720
So we had only last week, I think it was,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.680
the Delivery Plan
- Yeah.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.890
- for Recovering Access to Primary Care.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.040
Prior to that we'd had the
three-year Delivery Plan

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.610
for Maternity and Neonatal
Services published.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.150
And only a very few weeks before that

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.550
a Delivery Plan for Recovering,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.370
Urgent and Emergency Care services.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:49.110
And we'd also provided an
update to the delivery schedule

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.170
and plan for tackling elective backlog.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.760
Again, that was also published
back in I think February,

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:57.873
or February time.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.630
Outstanding and hopeful that
it will be published soon,

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.030
it actually is the
long-term workforce plan.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.120
Again, my compliments
to the executive team

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.390
for what is a very good piece of work,

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.520
a very good plan,

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.190
and capacitizing that once
published, I think, will be

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.700
a major step forward in
terms of our confidence

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.250
in delivery of the various services.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.913
But my thanks to the
team for what really is

00:05:24.913 --> 00:05:29.253
a very good, a really strong
foundational piece of work.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.450
Worth reflecting that we met as a Board,

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.310
not only this morning in private session,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.440
but we met a couple of weeks or so ago,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.910
10 days or so ago, to
reflect on strategic issues.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.310
And we looked at three areas,

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.530
one of which we're going to cover again

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.430
in some degree later on today
on technology and innovation,

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.160
which Tim Ferris will lead us through.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.900
But we looked at capital and estates,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.460
as there's one particular topic.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.400
We looked at technology and innovation.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.840
And we also looked at prevention

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.040
and the discussion on prevention,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.780
and our strategic conversation

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.773
moved across to consider
primary and community.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.160
I think it was a really, really strong

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.490
and very helpful conversation,

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.040
and a very good engagement,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.040
not just with the senior
executive but executive teams.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.473
And it's part of a move
that we have a movement

00:06:22.473 --> 00:06:25.920
that we have to increase
the strategic content

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:26.970
of the Board's agenda.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.090
So yes, it's our duty to challenge

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.380
and support the challenge

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.970
and support the operational performance

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.170
and the day-to-day activities of the NHS,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:41.370
but also actually to
increasingly to look at

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:45.690
strategic priorities
and to focus very much

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.063
on how we are going to deliver those.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.770
Just a few of the meetings and visits

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.620
that I've made over the past few weeks.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.360
I had a very good session,
actually, very good meeting

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.320
with Patricia Hewitt earlier
on this week actually,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.210
which was a very good catch up with her,

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.830
after her very good review,
meetings with Treasury.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.390
Again, very helpful conversations,

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.910
again talking around Capital and CDEL,

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.620
and the long-term workforce plan.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.340
And also actually an interesting momentum

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.800
in the way technology
spend is now increasing,

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.660
categorised not just
for the health service,

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.000
but actually across government more widely

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.010
and into the commercial world

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.380
with the technology industry
of that really moving

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:31.140
much more in its engagement
to revenue expenditure

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.340
rather than selling kit,

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.600
which is bought as capital.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.200
So it does give allocation challenges

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.120
to customers of technology,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.280
in terms of all their
spending money and capital,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.810
or their spending revenue budget.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.505
And that clearly is something

00:07:45.505 --> 00:07:47.130
we need to think about.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.060
I had a very good meeting

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.150
with a number of the
other Health ARB Chairs,

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:52.640
where we discussed a number of points

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:54.630
of actually of common interest.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.690
And I was delighted to be
invited by NHS providers

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:00.450
to do a sort of a Q&amp;A over dinner.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.220
Helpfully over dinner

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.430
with a number of trust and system chairs.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:09.783
And that was a really good conversation.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.840
We regularly, as the Board know,

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:15.750
host what are called stakeholder breakfast

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:16.980
or round tables.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.310
And Chris Hopson, thank
you for your initiative

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.317
in doing that, and also in hosting those,

00:08:22.317 --> 00:08:24.600
and we've had two of those recently.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.390
About three weeks ago we
had an excellent gathering

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:29.220
on the topic of prevention,

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.700
and it brings together very
senior and relevant expertise

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.370
from around health, from social care,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.150
from broader community
to look at that topic.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.850
And then only earlier this week,

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.620
it was probably yesterday
morning actually,

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:47.787
we had a very good round
table on primary care.

00:08:47.787 --> 00:08:51.090
And clearly that's a really
important part of the system,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:51.923
that going back
- Yeah.

00:08:51.923 --> 00:08:53.880
- to my comments around strategic thinking

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.880
is something that we really
are very focused on pursuing.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.480
And then Amanda and I
with Tim had a meeting

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.180
with UK Research and Innovation,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:07.830
happening on Monday morning

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.410
with the chief exec and chairman.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:11.670
Again, it was a good conversation

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.310
around the landscape and the
number of different bodies

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.010
through which actually
innovation and research

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.250
is presented effectively
in the end to the NHS.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.260
And our question about
how do we prioritise,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.440
how do we scale from what is
sort a multiple set of demands

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.160
made upon us via a multiple number

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.479
of different research councils
and other institutions,

00:09:32.479 --> 00:09:35.820
as well as private sector entities.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.020
And then lastly, I had a
very good visit last week

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.530
to Hertfordshire Partnership
University Foundation Trust,

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.870
where I was hosted by
Sarah Betteley, the chair,

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.000
and the chief executive, Karen Taylor,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.721
and visited a range of services

00:09:49.721 --> 00:09:51.930
and assessment and treatment centre,

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.510
and also separately a medium secure unit.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.360
It was really, it gave
me very helpful, again,

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.360
frontline insights, which
I find truly helpful,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:06.480
and my thanks to them for hosting me.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.270
So I'm happy to pause if anyone has

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.760
any comments or questions
on anything I've said

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.910
by way of introduction.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:17.160
And if not, I'm going to
hand to the chief executive

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.040
for a much more cogent update.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.800
I think than my opening, but Amanda.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.690
- Thank you, thank you Richard.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:29.160
So actually I've got a fair
range of things to cover,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.230
if I may, as part of my update.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.430
But I'd like to start by just
talking about our ongoing work

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.610
to create a new NHS England.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.390
So we are of course now
legally the new NHS England

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.720
having completed the merger
with Health Education England

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:47.190
at the start of April,

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:50.520
and previously the
merger with NHS Digital.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.470
So I would just like to put on record,

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.290
again my thanks to
everybody who was involved

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:58.599
in that part of the process.

00:10:58.599 --> 00:11:00.900
The other part of the process
that was the legal bit,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.300
and there was an awful lot
of work around that though.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.790
The next part and the other
part is really about moving

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.790
towards an organisation
that is the right size,

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.250
and is also focused on the right things

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.970
to support the wider NHS to succeed.

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:19.230
And there has been a huge
amount of work and engagement

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.900
to get us towards that
future design, which will be,

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:23.730
as we've said many times before,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.470
at least 30% and up to 40%
smaller in headcount terms

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:32.610
than the combined size of the
three previous organisations.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.190
As part of that, we are in
the process of supporting

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:38.760
over 1,200 colleagues to
leave the organisation,

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.810
as part of the first round
of voluntary redundancies.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.420
And we have known, I think
it's worth saying, you know,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.130
we've known that this would be

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.710
a very unsettling time for colleagues,

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.980
and that was why we had set
out when we first embarked

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:55.410
on this process to ensure
that everyone should know

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.460
what their future status would
be by September this year.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.650
But unfortunately, for
a number of reasons,

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.347
it's now clear we won't
be able to achieve that

00:12:04.347 --> 00:12:07.020
for every colleague,
whilst also maintaining

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.480
a fair and a proper process.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.780
So this afternoon, following
a decision we made as a Board

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.150
earlier today, a number
of executive colleagues

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.570
have had conversations
with their teams support,

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.850
just talking through and walking through

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.723
the changes to the timetable,
for some directorates.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:29.850
We thought long and hard about
how to rephase the process

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.000
in a way that is as quick as possible,

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.700
but fair for staff.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.940
Secondly, that gives
directorates time to take,

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:40.830
and this is a really important point,

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.890
just full account of the
significant level of feedback

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.900
that colleagues have given
as part of consultation.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.606
'cause the point of consulting
is that you want to,

00:12:49.606 --> 00:12:51.270
you know, you want to
hear back from people,

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.260
and then we need to make sure

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.870
that we really have taken
on board that feedback

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:57.090
in the design work and
as part of the process.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.430
So giving time for that.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.800
Third thing is avoiding consultations

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:02.910
over the summer holidays,

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.280
and decisions made being
made and communicated

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:06.450
very close to Christmas.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.550
And again, that's part
of the feedback we've had

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.760
about resetting a timetable
that again feels fair

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:15.600
to colleagues, but also,
as far as possible,

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.340
to avoid, and this is the final point,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.110
they need to extend it again.

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:21.603
So the new timetable for all staff

00:13:21.603 --> 00:13:24.840
that have been told what their future is

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:27.690
in the new organisation will now be set

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.300
at the end of March 2024.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.250
Although we do expect to be clear

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.460
that nine out of 10 will know
their status before Christmas.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.660
So that's a backstop
position for everybody.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:42.240
I think I just want to say on
the kind of personal behalf,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.250
but also on behalf I know
of all of my colleagues,

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.740
both executive and non-executive,

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:50.970
really aware that this has
been a very difficult process

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.240
for many colleagues, and
also that as a collective,

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:55.440
as part of the executive team,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:58.050
we would say we know we
haven't always got it right.

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:00.780
So, we absolutely all regret the need

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.220
to prolong the process further.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:03.810
And I know all of the execs here,

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.490
and our regional colleagues
who are also part

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:06.810
of our executive team,

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:08.550
of course, are completely committed

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:12.540
to ensuring that continued
engagement with their teams

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.400
to ensure people do get
the support they need

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:15.990
over the coming weeks and months.

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.190
But in saying all of that,

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:19.620
I think we would all
recognise that this is

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.660
an extremely difficult process,

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.330
and that is difficult for individuals,

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:26.820
and it's difficult for the
organisation as a whole,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.860
and we're incredibly grateful
for people's patience

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.580
and engagement, not just
over the last months,

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.770
but as we keep going and as we work now

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.770
on some of the real
opportunities in this process,

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.020
which are about creating
the organisation we need

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:42.930
for the future and putting
behind that the right culture,

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:46.423
the right ways of working so
that people can thrive here.

00:14:46.423 --> 00:14:50.967
- Can I, Amanda, just
interrupt you if I might,

00:14:50.967 --> 00:14:53.320
- Of course.
- just to sort of add

00:14:55.679 --> 00:14:58.537
my thanks and appreciation,

00:15:00.152 --> 00:15:02.553
and to you and the executive team,

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.500
and Steve Russell in particular

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.943
leading in the operationalization of this.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.270
And also my,

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.430
and on behalf of the Board
actually appreciation

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:17.160
of the whole of NHS England as combined,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.010
including NHS Digital and HEE,

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.440
as well as Improvement and, NHSX.

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.520
And it's my appreciation
of the staff and the team

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:29.670
actually for the fact that whilst

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.780
we go through a period of some
real uncertainty for people,

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.310
actually they're unstinted

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:40.310
in their commitment and their work effort,

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:43.530
and their delivery
actually on behalf of NHS,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.540
even though it's a period
of great uncertainty.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.080
And it is, and we often forget

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:50.580
just how significant an exercise,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:54.007
goodness, a significant a step this is,

00:15:54.007 --> 00:15:56.880
you know, coming at this NHS England

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.920
from the private sector.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.660
So what have we embarked upon here,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:01.950
and what has been delivered here?

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:05.910
You know, we've essentially merged with,

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:08.400
merged three organisations together,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.680
one of which had already
merged with another one,

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.237
so Improvement into England.

00:16:12.237 --> 00:16:16.410
And now we've merged with NHS Digital,

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:20.580
and now more recently with
Health Education England.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:22.860
And that brings around, I'll
get the numbers wrong Steve,

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:27.210
but broadly more than over 24,000 roles.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:28.770
And not only are we merging,

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.140
as the executive team leading
the merger of those operations

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:34.320
and maintaining their performance,

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.790
but also then reducing the headcount

00:16:38.315 --> 00:16:40.980
by a really thoughtful and cogent design.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:45.980
So how should the head office
of the NHS be reconfigured

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.603
and still engage properly
through the regions,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.200
into the ICSs, into the trusts, et cetera,

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.240
how should it be redesigned?

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:55.073
And to do that,

00:16:55.073 --> 00:16:59.430
taking between 30 and
40% of the headcount out.

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:02.070
In any private sector organisation.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:05.220
this is a very, very material exercise

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:07.200
that has been undertaken here.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.930
So I, you know, wholly understanding

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:11.640
and giving great appreciation

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.250
to all the staff who go
through this uncertainty,

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:15.690
I think the Board on behalf of Board

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.460
just to express our appreciation

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:20.190
for their continued diligence, and support

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.190
and engagement, and also our appreciation

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:25.080
to the executive team for the
way in which you have managed

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:26.850
this really quite complex exercise.

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:29.790
- Yeah, thank you
Richard, much appreciated.

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:31.950
And I think I would just
add to that, if I may,

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:32.783
not to prolong this,

00:17:32.783 --> 00:17:36.120
but to add my thanks again
particularly to members

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.010
of the executive team,

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.620
but also as you say to those
colleagues who are working

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:44.190
within this organisation
because it takes both sides.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.510
You know, we've got to be honest,

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.220
as I say that we haven't
always got things right.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:50.220
We're looking to learn and
change and improve the processes

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.607
as we go, but we will need to
work with people to do that.

00:17:52.607 --> 00:17:56.850
And we really value colleagues
continued just engagement,

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:59.370
and as you say, unstinting focus

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:02.670
actually on continuing to do
what is right for the NHS,

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:04.770
despite all of the rest of the change

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:06.120
that's going on around them.

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.070
And talking about, just if I
may then moving on to the NHS.

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:11.940
So of course what we've been dealing with,

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.700
just talking for a moment
about some of the challenges,

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:17.520
internally, externally, as it were

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:21.660
within the wider NHS of which
we are of course a component.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:26.550
There's been a huge amount
of disruption recently

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:28.470
caused by industrial action.

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:31.320
So over the last six weeks
we've had extended periods

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:35.220
of action by junior doctors
in early April RCN members,

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.480
end of April, beginning of May,

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:40.407
and also Unite members and see come in,

00:18:40.407 --> 00:18:44.550
and SCAS for a day in early May.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:47.760
So what that has meant in
practise is just in that period,

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.990
that six week period,
212,000 patient appointments

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.690
across all types of care were impacted,

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.910
which brings the total
number of appointments

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:59.940
needed to be rescheduled
since industrial action began

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.570
back in December, now to
more than half a million.

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.900
As colleagues know across the NHS

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:09.300
people have worked incredibly hard

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:10.770
to get those appointments rescheduled,

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:11.603
as quickly as possible,

00:19:11.603 --> 00:19:14.190
and to minimise the
disruption to patients.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.963
And again, I think we should
just be really grateful

00:19:17.963 --> 00:19:22.020
and put our thanks on record
to colleagues across the NHS,

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:25.080
all colleagues who have
worked so hard to make sure

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:29.040
that patients' care has
been disrupted to a minimum.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:32.700
But saying that, you know,
clearly that sort of volume

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.007
is not an easy thing for people to manage,

00:19:34.007 --> 00:19:37.110
and it has put an awful lot of additional,

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:40.620
I think just additional work

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:43.410
for people in the frontline
to keep managing that,

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:44.910
as well as colleagues here.

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:46.380
So where are we now?

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.300
As you know, staff council,

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.490
NHS Staff Council returned a majority vote

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:52.650
in favour of accepting the
agenda for change offer

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.330
on the second of May.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.490
So we have been working with
the Department of Health

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.950
and Social Care to action
the one-off payments,

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:02.130
and the uplift that flow from that.

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:04.770
That hasn't however resolved the agenda

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.040
for change issue entirely
because the RCN and Unite,

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.000
again, as colleagues know,

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.580
remain in active dispute
following a rejection

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:14.640
of that deal by their members.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:16.440
The government also remains in talks

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.660
with the BMA Junior Doctors Committee,

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.880
as well as the Consultants Committee,

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.030
the latter launching a ballot
of their members this week,

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:25.830
so that was the 15th of May.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:29.910
So our team continue to
work then with the wider NHS

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:32.790
to help mitigate the impact of
any further periods of action

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:34.740
as far as we possibly can.

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:37.380
And as we've said before,
and as I've just said,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.000
you know, every time we go through this

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.070
it does become more difficult,

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:44.040
even though I think collectively
we get better at planning.

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:48.143
Actually just the
resilience of the whole NHS

00:20:48.143 --> 00:20:50.163
is more challenged and more stretched.

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:53.550
Worth saying that
consultants taking action,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.350
which hasn't happened yet would of course

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:58.770
be a new, and I think, an
extremely difficult challenge

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:01.740
to manage because it's true
for some groups of staff,

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.060
but certainly true for consultants.

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:05.610
There are no other staff
groups who can cover

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.320
the work that they do.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:12.120
So we have continued and, you
know, we'll continue to do so,

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.690
but since December continue
to just encourage all parties

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:18.990
to talk constructively so
that hopefully we can avoid

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.273
any further impacts for patients.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:23.280
Do you want to say
anything more about that,

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:24.130
or happy for me to carry on?

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:25.020
- Go ahead, carry on.
- Carry on?

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:26.103
- Yeah.
- Okay, so,

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:30.630
I feel like I'm in danger
of just going through a list

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:31.710
of challenges at the moment,

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:35.700
but something that a little
more positively I think.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:39.030
Covid, so Covid has been a,

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:43.380
I think, well, an
international health emergency,

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:47.010
the like of which certainly
we haven't experienced

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:47.843
in our lifetime,

00:21:47.843 --> 00:21:50.850
and I hope we won't have
to experience again,

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:52.440
but we will be aware again,

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:54.090
colleagues will be aware
that earlier this month

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:56.340
the World Health Organisation
declared that Covid

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:59.490
is now an established
and ongoing health issue,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.140
and therefore no longer constitutes

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.993
a public health emergency
of international concern.

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:08.233
Covid is still a very, and
it's worth us remembering this,

00:22:08.233 --> 00:22:11.400
a very significant presence
and pressure for the NHS.

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:14.400
The latest statistics show we still had

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.740
over 3,500 people in hospital

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.203
with the virus on the 11th of May.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:23.610
So as such there is still very much a need

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.140
for a vaccination programme,

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:27.030
and we should just
acknowledge the great work

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.100
done by the team yet again, and delivering

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:32.100
almost three million
spring boosters so far.

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:36.480
But we are now at a point
in which we can stand down

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.960
our formal incident footing and transition

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:42.780
to managing Covid as a
business as usual part

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.240
of what the NHS does
in the way we do with,

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:47.370
for example, seasonal flu.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:50.040
So we have been in an official incident

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.490
since the 30th of January, 2020.

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:58.260
That is more than 1,200 days
of exceptionally hard work

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.330
by a huge number of people,

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:02.250
many colleagues around the table,

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:06.450
but also our EPR teams here
and across the country,

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:07.980
our volunteers, nationally,

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.690
and regionally and across the country,

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.690
and many other teams who've
been working on specific topics,

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:14.880
and in systems and in providers.

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:16.447
So again, I'm sure the whole Board

00:23:16.447 --> 00:23:19.740
will want to take this
opportunity to just thank everyone

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.590
once again for their
dedication and professionalism.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:25.860
Every single person has made
a very real contribution

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:28.170
to that NHS-wide effort.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:32.820
So on that, we will be writing,

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:34.020
David Sloman and I,

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.720
two colleagues in the wider NHS today

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:39.690
to say that we will now be moving away

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:41.880
from our formal incident footing

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:42.951
around Covid,
- Yeah.

00:23:42.951 --> 00:23:45.120
and we will be standing down

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:48.000
the instant management
arrangements for Covid,

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:49.290
and we'll describe in the letter

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.660
what that means in practical terms.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:54.510
But I think hopefully what
that will allow us to do

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:57.840
is recognise that whilst
it's still with us

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:59.430
with all the challenges it brings,

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:02.730
actually we are firmly
now focused on recovery.

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:05.340
So Steve said right at
the beginning of this,

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:08.280
it was going to be five
years that the Covid pandemic

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.320
would be a challenge
for us for five years.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:12.180
So we might be out of that acute phase,

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:14.700
but let's be under no illusion
we're three years through.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:16.710
So we've got two more years at least

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.250
of really I think living
with the recovery challenge.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:21.990
And we don't have a crystal ball,

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:23.280
so we don't know what might happen.

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:24.150
This is a virus,

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:27.000
it could well be that we'll
be having this conversation

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.337
again in a different
way later in the year.

00:24:29.337 --> 00:24:31.110
And I'm sure this won't be the last time

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.330
we talk about Covid
around this Board table.

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:36.840
But as of today, I think
a bit of good news perhaps

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:38.610
that we do feel we can now stand down

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.610
that formal incident status.

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:42.060
- Mm-hmm.
- A few more things,

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:44.580
if I may, you've already
mentioned it, Richard,

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:46.860
but I did just want to add
to what you've just said

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:47.970
about the publication

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:49.770
of the primary care
- Yip.

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:50.850
- access recovery plan

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:53.940
'cause talking about recovery
and what our priorities are

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.840
in moving from that acute
phase of Covid response

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.700
through now very much
to the recovery phase.

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:01.890
Well, of course primary care is absolutely

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:03.570
at the heart of that,

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:05.850
so thank you particularly to Amanda

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:07.203
and to her colleagues for all of the work

00:25:07.203 --> 00:25:09.840
that they did on pulling together

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:13.393
a plan which, you know, has been I think

00:25:13.393 --> 00:25:14.820
a really intensive effort

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.230
over a period of time
- Yeah.

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:19.230
- to get it developed, get it published.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.020
But just to state the
really obvious point,

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.707
of course the plan is only,

00:25:23.707 --> 00:25:25.890
you know, well, I say the first step,

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.440
it's been a step that's been
iterating for some time,

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.540
but the real focus now of course is

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:31.373
on implementation
- Delivery.

00:25:31.373 --> 00:25:33.660
- and delivery of that plan.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.210
And the reason that matters
is 'cause we know the access

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:40.117
to primary care is absolutely
the thing that the public,

00:25:40.117 --> 00:25:42.254
you know, really care about.

00:25:42.254 --> 00:25:43.860
And when you think about
the volume of people

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:46.350
who are treated as, you
often say Richard, you know,

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:49.860
well over a million people a
day treated in primary care

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.710
seeing a GP or a member
of the primary care team.

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:55.350
It is no surprise that that
is such an important priority

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:57.411
for the public and
therefore of course for us.

00:25:57.411 --> 00:25:58.290
- Yes, this is.

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:00.360
I always keep going
back and I do say that,

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:03.450
so sort of almost one, well,
1.1 million people every day,

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:04.740
every weekday.
- Yeah.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:05.580
Yeah.
- The population

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:09.813
of England's 57 million and yet
1.1 million people every day

00:26:09.813 --> 00:26:11.627
are being looked after in primary care,

00:26:11.627 --> 00:26:13.560
having appointments in primary care.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:16.740
It's remarkable really.
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:18.777
The other thing you mentioned earlier on,

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:21.150
you talked about urgent emergency care

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.200
access recovery plan,

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:25.050
but also the work that we did last year

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:27.120
to pull together the
elective recovery plan.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:28.620
So I just wanted to say
a few words on that,

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:30.960
not to cut across what
Sarah Jane will tell us

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:33.000
around performance a bit later on,

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.700
but it's just worth
taking a moment I think

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:38.820
because we had hit, as colleagues recall,

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.340
the first milestone on implementation

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.590
of the Elective Recovery
Plan back in July,

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:47.280
when we virtually eliminated
two year waits for treatment,

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:51.600
and we were hoping to
have achieved the same

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.300
for this March with
the virtual elimination

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:58.080
of 18 months wait for treatment.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:01.050
There has been just the most
enormous pressure on the NHS

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.390
as we know through an
unprecedented winter challenge.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:06.180
And then all of these other challenges,

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:09.300
not least industrial
action adding to that,

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.070
but as I say a huge testament

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.080
to the work of colleagues across the NHS,

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.030
enormous amount of focus,

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.810
and ingenuity and creativity
and determination.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.280
Which did mean that we,

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:23.910
last week when we published the figures,

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:25.980
they confirmed that at the end of March

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:27.840
the number of people
waiting over 18 months

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:31.410
had reduced to 10,737.

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:33.580
That is a reduction of over 90%

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:37.320
from the nearly 125,000 people

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:39.330
who were waiting at that point

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:40.830
in September 21.
- Yeah.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:44.730
- So, you know, so, would
we have liked? Of course.

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:48.180
Will we continue to try and
make sure that we go further?

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:50.937
Absolutely, and I know
colleagues across the NHS

00:27:50.937 --> 00:27:53.940
are hugely committed to
continuing to make progress,

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:57.420
not just to virtually eliminate 78 weeks,

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.880
but to get down to our next big milestone,

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:04.880
which is 65 weeks this time
next year or March next year.

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:07.643
But I just think we should take
a moment to thank colleagues

00:28:07.643 --> 00:28:10.380
for the just Herculean efforts

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:11.790
that have already gone into
- Yeah.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:16.790
- getting that 90% improvement
up to the end of March.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:21.600
So we continue to focus on
the elective recovery plan

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.760
and part of that now of course,

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.680
and Sarah Dobel want to talk about this,

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.080
is really driving the
outpatient transformation work,

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:29.970
which is written into the plan.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.010
But it's something that
becomes more and more important

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:35.160
as we start to think about
the further reductions

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:37.260
where more people are waiting

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.330
for outpatients and diagnostics

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.280
in the early parts of the pathway.

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:44.910
Whereas certainly once you
get to the very long waits,

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.760
vast majority of people there
are waiting for surgery.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:48.593
So it's a both end,

00:28:48.593 --> 00:28:49.426
as we go forward
- Yeah.

00:28:49.426 --> 00:28:52.260
- as these things usually are.

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:54.000
Two more things very quickly.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.104
Actually that's a lie, three more things,

00:28:56.104 --> 00:28:57.195
but the two,

00:28:57.195 --> 00:28:59.820
but two of them, the first is just to say,

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.110
of course you are right Richard.

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:03.960
The next big thing that we're
hoping for is the publication

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.220
of the long-term workforce plan

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:07.080
because if we're talking
about elective recovery,

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:08.720
we're talking about primary care recovery,

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:09.877
or we're talking about,

00:29:09.877 --> 00:29:12.030
you know, any of the
other ambitions we have

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:13.410
for the NHS now or in the future.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:16.350
We know we can do none of
that without our workforce.

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:21.180
So delighted that we
are, I hope now within

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:24.330
a very short timeframe of being
able to see that published.

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.414
And, again, I think that's
going to be quite a big moment

00:29:27.414 --> 00:29:29.820
for the NHS and the
challenge you've rightly set,

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:31.710
which is to think about delivery

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:34.350
is the right one as ever.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:39.350
- So just to kind of bring us back

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:43.050
to then other milestones
that we're looking at.

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.540
In the short term we've got

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:47.430
hopefully the Workforce plan shortly.

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:50.460
The other thing that is
definitely upon us shortly

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:53.220
is the NHS's 75th birthday

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:56.520
because that is coming
on the fifth of July,

00:29:56.520 --> 00:29:59.640
and we are looking to make,

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:02.160
I think sure that we have
taken the opportunity

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:04.260
around the 75th.

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:08.160
Indeed we're doing it in a
number of different ways.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.600
- Yeah.
- To celebrate

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.320
the 75 years at the NHS has been

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:15.590
at the heart of this,

00:30:15.590 --> 00:30:19.140
of the public services
that this country provides.

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:23.160
And also to take a moment
to reflect on the changes

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.800
that we've seen over the last 75 years,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:27.300
where we find ourselves now,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.310
and of course to look ahead to the future.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.970
So we are going to be doing
lots of events around the 75th,

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:36.120
giving people a chance to contribute

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.560
in lots of different ways.

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:40.230
And the centrepiece will be a
service at Westminster Abbey

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:42.750
on the anniversary itself.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:43.917
It's a brilliant opportunity,

00:30:43.917 --> 00:30:47.880
the 75th I think also just to
make perhaps younger people

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:50.670
aware of the sorts of
opportunities that there are

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:52.890
for careers in the NHS.

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:56.190
So I went back to my old school in Durham,

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:57.120
a couple of weeks ago,

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:00.360
just to talk to actually an
incredibly impressive group

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:02.677
of kids about the NHS,

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:06.990
and about the fact that there
are 350 different careers

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:09.660
in the NHS, many of them accessible

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.130
through things like apprenticeships,

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:13.140
as well as different routes into training,

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:16.021
the more traditional university route.

00:31:16.021 --> 00:31:17.340
So, something for everyone,

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:18.210
I think we would say,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:19.620
in the NHS,

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.960
and it was great actually
to have so much engagement,

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:24.457
so much enthusiasm from young people who,

00:31:24.457 --> 00:31:27.010
you know, were I think certainly

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:30.570
perhaps not aware of all of the roles,

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.660
and not aware of all the
opportunities that do exist.

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:35.610
But got to talk about the future as well.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:40.043
So we've got to look
back, think about what's,

00:31:40.043 --> 00:31:42.177
what's been the real success stories

00:31:42.177 --> 00:31:43.650
for the last 75 years.

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:46.020
We've got to be honest about
some of where we are now,

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:48.150
the challenges, but also
all the things we've got

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:48.983
in train,
- Yeah,

00:31:48.983 --> 00:31:50.850
- to meet those challenges,

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.340
and the very important
plans that we've got

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.290
now out and about to be published,

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.210
which will speak directly to that.

00:31:57.210 --> 00:31:59.370
But I think we've also got to
give ourselves the opportunity

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:02.400
to look ahead and to
celebrate some of the things

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:05.760
that the NHS continues to
do to innovate and change,

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:08.430
and has done really for its last 75 years

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:10.110
to make sure we are fit for the needs

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:11.250
of different generations.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.860
So just this morning we
have been able to announce

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.590
a new life extending combination therapy

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:21.420
for hundreds of women with
advanced endometrial cancer

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:23.400
that will be made available
across the country.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:26.340
And we've been able to use
our commercial capabilities

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:29.220
to negotiate with two
separate manufacturers

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:30.660
because clinical trials have shown

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:34.470
that when these two
products are used together,

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:37.500
they can almost double the
time taken for advanced cancer

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:40.830
to progress compared to the
current chemotherapy treatment.

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:45.830
So that's just yet another
happens to be today opportunity

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:49.650
to announce another life extending

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:52.080
and another life-changing treatment

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.780
that will be available on the NHS.

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:57.930
So tomorrow, to give a sneak preview,

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.180
we will be announcing a new partnership

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:02.820
with NHS Blood and Transplant

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:05.700
to roll out a world first programme

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:07.950
of genetic blood matching testing

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:11.310
for sickle cell disease and
for thalassemia patients.

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:14.340
So in time that could help
us provide donor blood

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:17.310
for transfusions that much
more accurately matches

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:19.200
those patients who need them,

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:20.850
reducing the risk of side effects,

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:22.320
which in some cases of course can make

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:24.570
people's conditions worse.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:27.330
So both of those new
investments in patient care

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:29.010
and outcomes, really good examples,

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:30.870
I think, of how quickly the NHS

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:34.440
can also move to ensure
patients can benefit

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:35.830
from these new technologies,

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:37.230
- Yeah.
- new drugs,

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:38.880
and get them out to those people

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:40.500
who really need them.

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:43.440
So we set ourselves a challenge.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:46.380
We'll talk about it when
Tim talks about technology

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:48.030
to make sure that we've got

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:52.020
not just a clear opportunities

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:54.360
with new drugs and new tests,

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:57.420
but also that we're really
using the new technologies

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:00.447
and the new opportunities that
are coming through that way.

00:34:00.447 --> 00:34:02.820
And I'm delighted that we're
going to be able to talk a bit

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:05.220
about what we're doing
there as well later on.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:09.000
So 75th an opportunity
for us to get together,

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:09.833
and we're using it.

00:34:09.833 --> 00:34:12.510
I just want to, again, finally
say the very last thing,

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:16.050
thank the NHS Assembly
who are doing some work

00:34:16.050 --> 00:34:19.020
talking to both colleagues within the NHS

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:22.290
and partners to help inform us

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:26.070
and inform the Board
about how other people

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:27.533
would see that journey for the NHS

00:34:27.533 --> 00:34:31.080
and the things they care about
both now and into the future.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.434
So really looking forward
to getting that work back

00:34:33.434 --> 00:34:35.400
from the NHS Assembly.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:36.400
- Thank you, Amanda.

00:34:37.355 --> 00:34:38.497
Just that last point actually about,

00:34:38.497 --> 00:34:40.140
you know, where we've come from

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:42.210
and where we currently are facing,

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:43.170
you know, real challenges

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:47.880
built to where we might
envision or imagine the future,

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:48.870
the NHS does,

00:34:48.870 --> 00:34:50.250
as you really showed me,

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:53.820
- it continues to evolve
and change all the time,

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.033
bringing in evermore innovation actually.

00:34:56.033 --> 00:34:57.690
And so, actually Tim,

00:34:57.690 --> 00:34:58.860
we're building up your session

00:34:58.860 --> 00:34:59.760
later on this afternoon
- We are.

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:02.793
- quite strongly
actually, but that's okay.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:06.450
Okay if I move on?
- Yeah.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:08.130
- Sarah Jane,

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:11.370
- will you walk us through
Operational Performance Update?

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:12.600
- Oh, thank you. Thank you, Chair.

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.850
It's a pleasure to join
you this afternoon.

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:16.290
So I think the first thing to say,

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:18.990
operational delivery in the
NHS and the every period

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:21.480
that you reflect on it's got
its own unique challenges,

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:22.980
and the one that I'm
going to report on now

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:24.360
principally the month of April.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:26.850
Amanda's already said a few things about,

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:30.270
but we had a backdrop of
significant industrial action,

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:32.430
at a time when we are working really hard

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.370
to recover our Urgent and
Emergency Care elective,

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:37.380
and other services.

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:39.780
I think going into the
period we really had

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:42.210
three clear strategic objectives.

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:44.490
The first of those was
to keep patients safe,

00:35:44.490 --> 00:35:46.500
both those that were
already in our services,

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:49.890
but those that needed to access
Urgent and Emergency Care.

00:35:49.890 --> 00:35:52.620
The second was to do
everything we possibly could

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.780
to keep going with our elective programme,

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:56.880
given we know how long many
people have been waiting

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:00.090
for surgery and how much they
rely on those appointments.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.280
And then the third is to do
everything we possibly could

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:04.620
to maintain good working relationships

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.290
between all groups given
the pressures and tensions

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:10.290
that people were under during that period.

00:36:10.290 --> 00:36:11.820
And I just remind people again,

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:14.760
I know we talk about
this a lot as a Board,

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.490
that the level of need that people

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.010
accessing our services have at the moment

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:21.210
is very significant.

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.030
So we can talk about
demand in terms of numbers,

00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:27.840
but we also need to really
recognise the complexity,

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:31.110
the comorbidities, the pressure
that many of our services

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:34.680
are under because of the all
different sort of treatments

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:37.080
that people coming forward
to services require,

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:40.560
and on top of that the
complexities of Covid as well.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:42.410
So I think when I just share with you

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:45.450
a few numbers against that background,

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:48.120
it's all the more remarkable
what we managed to do

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:49.920
in the month of April.

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:52.110
So I think the two things
I would want to highlight

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:55.110
for Urgent and Emergency
Care is that we had

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:58.893
one of our best months for
ambulance response times

00:36:58.893 --> 00:37:02.280
that that we've had since
before the pandemic.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:05.340
We improved through April
and our published figure

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:08.190
was 28 minutes and 35 seconds,

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:11.310
which would put us inside
of the operational standard

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:12.990
that we've set ourselves this year.

00:37:12.990 --> 00:37:15.090
We know we've got further to go in time.

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:16.860
We know that that even that is too long,

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:20.910
but that is the challenge
that we set out for ourselves.

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:25.380
And we achieved 74.6% for A&amp;E performance,

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:27.480
which again is remarkable really,

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:30.900
if you think about where we
were in the previous year.

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.930
So I think we have to
say that was a remarkable

00:37:33.930 --> 00:37:35.310
set of achievements.

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:39.240
I recognise that many people
worked extraordinary hours.

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:41.190
I meet with doctors a lot in my role,

00:37:41.190 --> 00:37:44.640
a lot of tired faces of
people that work night shifts

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:47.340
and other things to make that possible,

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:50.130
but I think we need to recognise
what was achieved there.

00:37:50.130 --> 00:37:53.220
I think we also need to note
that that was still done

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:55.860
against a background
of high bed occupancy.

00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:58.650
We were still running it around 95%,

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:01.290
and we've still got
significant and ongoing issues

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:03.570
with moving people out of hospital,

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:05.160
onto the next right service for them

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:06.870
when they're ready to leave.

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:08.940
However, I think some of the
work that we've been doing

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:11.130
on discharge and in particular our focus

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:12.780
on long length of stay,

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:15.720
we've started to see since
January for the first time

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.060
that we're starting to
reverse some of the trends.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:21.420
Our length of stay has been
growing quite dramatically

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:23.670
through Covid and beyond.

00:38:23.670 --> 00:38:26.130
We had actually increased length of stay

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:29.460
by almost a day and a half
over an 18 month period.

00:38:29.460 --> 00:38:31.800
And for the first time
we've stabilised that

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:34.620
and start to see that drop slightly now.

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:36.180
I think we need to watch what,

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:37.710
always cautious in operations

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:39.930
when something good happens
that you're going to lose it

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.250
in the next month.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:42.990
But I think we understand
some of the reasons

00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:44.250
why that's happened,

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:47.430
and we hope that we can
continue to replicate that.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:49.020
And then likewise, as Amanda said,

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:50.790
really given the pressures

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:51.900
that everybody was under,

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:56.040
I think to continue with the
level of elective operating

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:59.130
that we did, we know that
those decisions were made

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:01.020
on a trust by trust, ward by ward,

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:03.690
people making them in
the run up to protect

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:06.060
all of the procedures
that they possibly could.

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.610
But I think to the end of March position

00:39:08.610 --> 00:39:12.240
to be left with the 10,737 cohort,

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.580
whilst again we would want
that number to be lower

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:16.140
is an impressive achievement,

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:20.970
and then likewise the 62 day
cancer backlog fell as well.

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:23.340
So I think again that's a huge achievement

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:24.783
and testimony to the team.

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.690
Of course as we go into May,

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:29.130
we know we've got some challenges.

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:32.430
We've had a couple of
weekends with bank holidays,

00:39:32.430 --> 00:39:35.490
which always makes it
challenging for us to manage

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:40.490
when it's a four day working
week for some of our services.

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:44.400
But we've managed to hold
on I think to quite a lot

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:45.270
of the improvements.

00:39:45.270 --> 00:39:47.820
The A&amp;E performance is slightly down,

00:39:47.820 --> 00:39:49.980
and the response times are slightly up.

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:52.230
The elective programme looks
like it's coming back strong

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:53.063
into its own.

00:39:53.063 --> 00:39:54.990
But I think we just need to
keep a really careful eye,

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:56.490
and we do that every single day,

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:59.550
and obviously teams are
working on that constantly.

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:02.790
I think in terms of just
thinking about priorities

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:05.220
for the next couple of months.

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:06.220
We've talked about the recovery plans,

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.150
we're going to talk about the
primary care one in a moment.

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:12.570
I think for Urgent and Emergency Care,

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:15.420
we've got the system
plans for how people plan

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:19.620
to get themselves ready for
winter earlier than ever before.

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:22.140
And clearly we'll be working
really closely with regions

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:25.800
and systems to see that
the additional capacity,

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:28.050
and the new models of care and innovation

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.683
actually come through in
time for what we all know

00:40:31.683 --> 00:40:33.870
will be challenging again.

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:35.640
And from an elective perspective,

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:39.210
we'll be put in additional
emphasis on outpatients,

00:40:39.210 --> 00:40:43.620
given the majority of people
waiting on our waiting lists

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:46.020
are actually waiting for
an outpatient appointment.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:48.210
And we know that there are
many places across the country

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:50.610
that are transforming the way
outpatients are delivered,

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.190
both from a new and follow up perspective,

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:54.510
and really have a focus on that,

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:56.400
and getting people answers to questions

00:40:56.400 --> 00:40:57.870
that they've got as quickly as possible,

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:01.710
and then moved on to the next
part of the pathway if needed.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:05.190
So that's where we've been
over the last few weeks.

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:07.530
As I say, this is cautious optimism.

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:09.150
We absolutely need to keep going,

00:41:09.150 --> 00:41:11.400
and we know we're going to have
tough days and weeks ahead,

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:12.990
but I think some of the things,

00:41:12.990 --> 00:41:15.117
the improvements and the
different ways of working

00:41:15.117 --> 00:41:16.557
are really starting to show now,

00:41:16.557 --> 00:41:18.753
and we've got quite a lot to be proud of.

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:24.690
- Thank you, let me open up for questions.

00:41:24.690 --> 00:41:26.163
Any questions? Andrew?

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:28.800
- So, thanks, Richard.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:30.930
So I think this is the best set of results

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:33.390
that I've seen post,

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:35.190
you know, on the run up to the pandemic,

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:36.960
and as we're coming out of the pandemic,

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:39.630
it looks really promising
to have made progress,

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:43.080
not on one front but
across several fronts.

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:44.730
And cancer is a really,

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:46.590
you know, staggering bit of good news.

00:41:46.590 --> 00:41:49.470
We're kind of moving the
dial significantly there,

00:41:49.470 --> 00:41:53.190
and the same with four hours
and the ambulance handoffs.

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:56.160
I suppose the big challenge
we've got going forward

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:59.790
is we've got lots of
good practise going on.

00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:02.970
How can we, how can NHS England add value,

00:42:02.970 --> 00:42:07.260
and spread, you know,
best practise at scale?

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:10.410
'Cause the NHS has always
got this reluctance

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:11.407
to adopt something that,

00:42:11.407 --> 00:42:14.880
you know, a trust next door is pursuing,

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:18.000
or the organisation down the road.

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.137
So, what are we doing to ensure that,

00:42:20.137 --> 00:42:22.890
you know, stuff goes really well?

00:42:22.890 --> 00:42:24.748
And you talked about outpatients,

00:42:24.748 --> 00:42:25.581
- Yeah.
- be good

00:42:25.581 --> 00:42:27.540
just to get a sense of, you know, flavour

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:30.780
in terms of how that's all progressing.

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.163
- Before you, let me see
- Yeah.

00:42:32.163 --> 00:42:33.390
- if I can gather some more.

00:42:33.390 --> 00:42:35.820
You might want to quickly jot

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:36.653
Andrew's questions.
- Yes.

00:42:36.653 --> 00:42:38.400
- Let me see if there
are any other comments

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:40.097
or questions for, so Ruth?

00:42:40.097 --> 00:42:42.300
- It was just a comment really.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:45.150
It was a tough 28 hours
during the strike action,

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:49.410
and I want to just pay tribute
to all those trust nurses,

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:53.340
ICB nurses, the regional chief nurses,

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:58.340
you know, all levels of
operational teams across the NHS,

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:01.380
really pulled out everything they could

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:04.470
to make it as safely as possible

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:08.130
for that 28 hours.

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:12.810
And indeed then get back
up some of those electives

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:14.910
on the Tuesday.

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:16.540
I just wanted to just quickly say

00:43:16.540 --> 00:43:19.020
a big thank you to Nicola Ranger

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:22.680
and some of the teams
working with me and my teams

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:24.690
about those national mitigations

00:43:24.690 --> 00:43:27.060
because that did make a difference to us

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:29.760
being able to deal with
this industrial action,

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.410
as safe as we could.
- Yeah.

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:32.660
- [Ruth] Thank you, chair.

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:38.550
- The one question just
to help me on my stats,

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:39.990
and I probably got them wrong,

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:43.320
but of the waiting list,
they let you backlog,

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:46.530
it's about 3% or just under, isn't it,

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:48.810
that are surgical procedures requiring

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:49.993
an overnight stay?
- That's right, yes.

00:43:49.993 --> 00:43:53.550
- And that's within about
18% of the waiting list,

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:56.280
that is requiring some surgical procedure,

00:43:56.280 --> 00:44:00.090
so on the same day, no stay required?

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:01.530
And then it's over 80% then,

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:03.450
which is predominantly outpatient.

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:04.283
Is that right?
- Yes.

00:44:04.283 --> 00:44:05.580
- Is that still the shape of the-

00:44:05.580 --> 00:44:07.050
- I would...

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:08.250
The last figures are
- The orders

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:09.083
of magnitude?
- going to be,

00:44:09.083 --> 00:44:09.916
'cause that if it's changed

00:44:09.916 --> 00:44:10.749
it would've only changed

00:44:10.749 --> 00:44:11.870
by a very small percentage.

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:12.900
- So that's absolutely,

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:14.730
so it shows you where the big balance of-

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:17.370
- [Richard] So your comments
on outpatients, I think were.

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:18.203
- Yeah, and the,

00:44:18.203 --> 00:44:20.250
I mean clearly we need to
focus on all areas of it,

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:22.230
but if we are really going
to get the waiting list

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:25.050
moved quickly, and get,
you know, many people

00:44:25.050 --> 00:44:26.490
going through their pathways,

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.760
the focus on outpatients is
the one that we are really

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:31.530
going to place over the next 12 months.

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:33.240
And we can see lots of
parts of the country,

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.710
people are absolutely doing this,

00:44:34.710 --> 00:44:37.080
but I think it's Andrew's
challenge about scale.

00:44:37.080 --> 00:44:39.270
Lots of people are having
to go at new ways of working

00:44:39.270 --> 00:44:40.103
in a particular place,
- Yeah.

00:44:40.103 --> 00:44:41.220
- in a particular specialty,

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:42.120
but we need to scale

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:44.738
that all-
- Or share

00:44:44.738 --> 00:44:46.713
the best working practise.

00:44:46.713 --> 00:44:48.217
- Should I go to Andrew?
- Please do.

00:44:48.217 --> 00:44:49.050
- Yeah, I mean I did.
- Yeah.

00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:51.000
- I see in many ways
that's the job, Andrew,

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:53.640
like the what part of it is
almost the easy bit, isn't it?

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:56.880
It's the how we do it and
how we collectively together

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.010
as a community of people
that all want to achieve

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:01.500
the same things here, which
is the important part.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:03.870
So I think we've got several
different roles in NHS England.

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:06.360
I think the first, we've
obviously got the recovery plan,

00:45:06.360 --> 00:45:09.630
but it's been really
clear what the things are

00:45:09.630 --> 00:45:11.970
in the recovery plans that actually work.

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:14.730
Like what are the things
that the organisations

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:16.980
that get the best performance do?

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.510
You know in Urgent and Emergency Care,

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:19.500
what is it that they're doing

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:22.500
about same day emergency care
that other places may not be?

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:25.380
Or what are they doing about
intermediate care on electives?

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:27.060
How are people looking

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:30.690
at patient directed follow-ups themselves

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:31.800
in a different way?

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:32.790
So there's lots of,

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:34.830
we need to be really clear
what those things are,

00:45:34.830 --> 00:45:35.663
and share them.

00:45:35.663 --> 00:45:37.020
I think we already do do some of that,

00:45:37.020 --> 00:45:38.970
but being a lot more sort of systematic

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:40.470
about the way we do that.

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:42.030
So in UEC for example,

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:44.790
we've got 15 products
that we've identified

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:46.680
that if you do these 15 things,

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:48.570
you are much more likely

00:45:48.570 --> 00:45:50.520
to have successful sort of emergency care,

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:53.640
and then it's the same across
all the programmes really.

00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:55.260
It's then networking people together,

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:58.560
bringing them together in
collaboratives improvement groups,

00:45:58.560 --> 00:46:01.530
so people can make those
changes and learn together.

00:46:01.530 --> 00:46:03.300
Now, for the majority of places

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:05.310
people want to work like that,

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:06.810
and with a bit of help and support

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:09.300
we can bring people together

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:11.670
where we know organisations
need more support.

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:15.207
It's about more clearly sort
of networking them together

00:46:15.207 --> 00:46:17.760
and getting them to learn from each other.

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:19.470
But I think we are really clear on this.

00:46:19.470 --> 00:46:22.350
We do need to achieve the targets,

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:24.270
and of course they're really important,

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:25.680
but all of the recovery plans,

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:27.690
and we'll hear this about
primary care in a moment,

00:46:27.690 --> 00:46:31.680
they are citizen offers and we
can't be picking and choosing

00:46:31.680 --> 00:46:33.240
which of those things that we want to do

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:35.010
in particular places.

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:38.100
If you are ill and in
need of help and support,

00:46:38.100 --> 00:46:39.800
you need to have that
service available to you

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:41.010
in your local areas.

00:46:41.010 --> 00:46:43.184
So it is a holistic offer that we do,

00:46:43.184 --> 00:46:46.530
and we are not just sort of
performance managing people

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:47.910
to meet some numbers.

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:50.550
We saying these things
together as a combination,

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:53.820
come and transform the way you
will get your elective care

00:46:53.820 --> 00:46:56.700
or your cancer care or your
urgent and emergency care,

00:46:56.700 --> 00:46:58.680
and we need to make
them happen everywhere.

00:46:58.680 --> 00:47:01.050
And that's what this year's
planning process has been about.

00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:03.480
Really just getting
people to absolutely focus

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:04.440
on all the areas,

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.780
and say what is possible in this place

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:08.880
for the citizens that we serve.

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:10.320
- Well, that's the big difference

00:47:10.320 --> 00:47:11.340
this time round,
- Yeah.

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:14.070
- compared to how we've done it

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:15.100
for the last kind of 10 years,
- Yeah.

00:47:15.100 --> 00:47:18.420
- which has been very much
around monitoring performance

00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:21.727
and saying to systems or providers,

00:47:21.727 --> 00:47:23.670
"It's not, your performance
isn't good enough,

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:25.800
you know, get better."

00:47:25.800 --> 00:47:28.290
Whereas I think this
is far more supportive,

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:32.023
far more collaborative and
in the end we'll get patients

00:47:32.023 --> 00:47:34.500
a much, much better
service, which is fantastic.

00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:35.550
It's really-
- Will be a little bit

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:36.750
of watching trajectories as well,

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:37.787
just to reassure.

00:47:37.787 --> 00:47:40.140
I mean we do need to achieve those goals

00:47:40.140 --> 00:47:42.087
'cause beneath those goals is a set of it,

00:47:42.087 --> 00:47:44.340
but it isn't just about
doing that attending cost,

00:47:44.340 --> 00:47:45.173
it's about doing it in
- Yeah,

00:47:45.173 --> 00:47:46.500
absolutely,
- in the right way.

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:48.750
- Thank you, so one of the things,

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:50.820
It's good updates on
operational performance,

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:52.590
and, oh, sorry, Mary,

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:53.550
- I'm so sorry.
- I apologise.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:54.430
- Thank you very much.
- Mary and David, sorry.

00:47:54.430 --> 00:47:56.010
- Yeah, yeah.
- I just

00:47:56.010 --> 00:47:56.910
- They-
- wanted to come in

00:47:56.910 --> 00:47:58.410
and congratulate you

00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:01.110
and everybody involved in the increase

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:03.390
in primary care appointments,

00:48:03.390 --> 00:48:06.750
and also the fact that so
many people are now being seen

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:10.843
within 14 days when they
have suspected cancer

00:48:10.843 --> 00:48:11.676
- Mm-hmm.
- Yeah.

00:48:11.676 --> 00:48:12.690
- because for every person

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:16.020
that does have cancer,
probably nine are relieved

00:48:16.020 --> 00:48:18.120
to discover that they don't.

00:48:18.120 --> 00:48:22.620
And I think we need to remember
that users of our service

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:24.630
don't all become long-term patients,

00:48:24.630 --> 00:48:25.463
- Yeah!
- Mm-hmm.

00:48:25.463 --> 00:48:27.570
- and we should really
be celebrating that.

00:48:27.570 --> 00:48:30.660
- Very good, thank you Mary. David?

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:33.840
- Sarah Jane, so I think
this emphasis you gave

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:36.390
