01:01:36.870 --> 01:01:40.050
with general practise that we have seen

01:01:40.050 --> 01:01:44.370
in the recent general
practise patient surveys.

01:01:44.370 --> 01:01:49.080
We've seen a really stark, a
stark drop in satisfaction.

01:01:49.080 --> 01:01:51.150
Actually we've seen no drop whatsoever

01:01:51.150 --> 01:01:52.710
in patient satisfaction

01:01:52.710 --> 01:01:55.380
with the care that they
receive in general practise.

01:01:55.380 --> 01:01:59.190
What we've seen is a really
marked drop in satisfaction

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:01.500
with people's ability to
get through to practises

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:03.090
to make an appointment.

01:02:03.090 --> 01:02:06.570
And so this plan is absolutely
about how we address

01:02:06.570 --> 01:02:10.950
that challenge that
patients are experiencing.

01:02:10.950 --> 01:02:13.470
How we make it easier for
people to get in touch

01:02:13.470 --> 01:02:15.900
with the practise, get the care we need,

01:02:15.900 --> 01:02:18.420
and support practise to be able to deliver

01:02:18.420 --> 01:02:20.580
what's actually a really marked increase

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:23.250
in demand from patients.

01:02:23.250 --> 01:02:24.810
Largely explained both

01:02:24.810 --> 01:02:29.610
by the still continuing backlog

01:02:29.610 --> 01:02:32.010
in people seeking care from the pandemic,

01:02:32.010 --> 01:02:33.780
but mostly by the fact that we have

01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:35.760
such a significant increase

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:40.673
in our over 65 population
over the past years.

01:02:42.060 --> 01:02:46.050
And the population over 65

01:02:46.050 --> 01:02:48.030
have on average about five times

01:02:48.030 --> 01:02:49.680
as many appointments a year,

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:51.081
as younger people do,

01:02:51.081 --> 01:02:56.081
and so they're the primary
driver of demand for care.

01:02:56.280 --> 01:02:59.880
As people live longer, they
have more long-term conditions,

01:02:59.880 --> 01:03:03.240
more what we call multimorbidity,

01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:06.420
and need more help from general practise.

01:03:06.420 --> 01:03:11.420
So this is designed to address
this in four main ways.

01:03:12.090 --> 01:03:14.880
First of which is to empower patients

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:19.140
to be able to access care
more easily themselves.

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:23.010
So we want to continue the work
we've done with the NHS app

01:03:23.010 --> 01:03:27.240
to enable patients in
over 90% of practises

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:29.370
to be able to see their
full medical records

01:03:29.370 --> 01:03:30.480
through the app,

01:03:30.480 --> 01:03:33.780
to book appointments, to
order repeat prescriptions,

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:38.520
to get information about how
to look after themselves,

01:03:38.520 --> 01:03:39.753
how to seek care,

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:44.280
and importantly to message
and receive messages

01:03:44.280 --> 01:03:45.603
from their practises.

01:03:46.680 --> 01:03:49.380
We are asking Integrated
Care Boards to expand

01:03:49.380 --> 01:03:51.930
the self-referral pathways

01:03:51.930 --> 01:03:54.120
that we've asked them to put in place

01:03:54.120 --> 01:03:55.350
by September of this year.

01:03:55.350 --> 01:03:58.350
They're largely for community services,

01:03:58.350 --> 01:04:01.710
of which patients generally
know what they need

01:04:01.710 --> 01:04:05.460
and often going to the GP for
referral is a pass through.

01:04:05.460 --> 01:04:08.430
So it's things like audiology
and hearing aid services.

01:04:08.430 --> 01:04:12.840
It's musculoskeletal
physio, it's podiatry,

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:15.333
it's community equipment services.

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:20.490
We are working with community pharmacy,

01:04:20.490 --> 01:04:24.630
which is a huge untapped

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:28.710
clinical resource where there
is significant capability

01:04:28.710 --> 01:04:31.170
to expand the role in
clinical service delivery

01:04:31.170 --> 01:04:33.090
that community pharmacy plays.

01:04:33.090 --> 01:04:35.590
And we're putting
significant investment in,

01:04:35.590 --> 01:04:39.930
up to 645 million pounds
over the next two years

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:43.020
to enable community pharmacy
to scale up their workforce

01:04:43.020 --> 01:04:45.060
and change their ways of working to take

01:04:45.060 --> 01:04:48.390
a much, much bigger role in
clinical service delivery.

01:04:48.390 --> 01:04:52.890
We'll do that by expanding
the blood pressure

01:04:52.890 --> 01:04:55.230
and oral contraceptive services

01:04:55.230 --> 01:04:57.750
available through community pharmacies.

01:04:57.750 --> 01:05:00.730
But importantly to launch
a pharmacy first scheme

01:05:01.770 --> 01:05:05.550
so that by the end of this
year community pharmacies

01:05:05.550 --> 01:05:08.070
will be able to supply
prescription-only medicines

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:09.630
via Patient Group Directions

01:05:09.630 --> 01:05:11.700
for seven high volume,

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:14.643
but generally low complexity conditions.

01:05:17.370 --> 01:05:19.590
We then want to move on to
implementing what we're calling

01:05:19.590 --> 01:05:22.050
modern general practise access.

01:05:22.050 --> 01:05:25.680
This is, we've all seen a lot in the press

01:05:25.680 --> 01:05:27.120
about telephone systems,

01:05:27.120 --> 01:05:28.860
but this is a combination

01:05:28.860 --> 01:05:33.690
of modern digital telephone systems,

01:05:33.690 --> 01:05:37.140
with full functionality
such as call diversion

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:40.083
and queuing call back functionality,

01:05:40.950 --> 01:05:44.370
which make it much,
much easier for patients

01:05:44.370 --> 01:05:45.690
planning to contact the practise,

01:05:45.690 --> 01:05:48.450
but also for practise to
manage the incoming calls,

01:05:48.450 --> 01:05:50.550
which often come as we
all know at eight o'clock

01:05:50.550 --> 01:05:51.960
in the morning.

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:54.900
Well, this on its own is not the solution.

01:05:54.900 --> 01:05:56.927
Telephone lines won't solve the problem,

01:05:56.927 --> 01:05:59.820
but it will improve things
for the 50% of practises

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:03.270
that are still on analogue phone lines,

01:06:03.270 --> 01:06:08.270
and in combination with
digital triage and flow tools

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:10.500
to manage incoming demand

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:13.140
and two-way communication platforms.

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:17.340
The full communication
functionality will allow practises

01:06:17.340 --> 01:06:19.680
to move to what we are terming

01:06:19.680 --> 01:06:21.180
modern general practise access,

01:06:21.180 --> 01:06:25.020
but essentially a digitally enabled way

01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:27.870
of managing incoming demands,

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:29.430
so that patients are directed

01:06:29.430 --> 01:06:32.163
to the most appropriate response for them.

01:06:33.600 --> 01:06:36.120
Probably only 10 to 15% of practises

01:06:36.120 --> 01:06:39.000
have all three of these
things in place at the moment.

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:42.210
The practises that do
have them are telling us,

01:06:42.210 --> 01:06:44.340
it's still very, very busy,

01:06:44.340 --> 01:06:46.440
but actually they are
managing their demand

01:06:46.440 --> 01:06:49.890
and we see higher patient satisfaction

01:06:49.890 --> 01:06:53.073
evident in those practises that
have these things in place.

01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:56.610
We recognise that changing
your model of delivery

01:06:56.610 --> 01:07:01.610
is a huge ask for what is
almost 6,500 general practises

01:07:01.710 --> 01:07:02.730
in the country.

01:07:02.730 --> 01:07:06.870
And so we are delivering
alongside the digital tools,

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:09.930
we're delivering training
in care navigation

01:07:09.930 --> 01:07:11.790
for general practise staff,

01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:15.030
but also funding transition
cover to allow practise

01:07:15.030 --> 01:07:17.280
the time and capacity to make the change

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:19.290
that's going to be required.

01:07:19.290 --> 01:07:24.290
It's a big OD exercise for
practises needing a lot of time,

01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:29.670
a lot of catch up with
appointment backlogs

01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:31.980
to start from scratch with a new model.

01:07:31.980 --> 01:07:34.260
And so we are recognising
that and funding that

01:07:34.260 --> 01:07:35.583
for all practises.

01:07:37.560 --> 01:07:39.780
This is not about making general practise

01:07:39.780 --> 01:07:41.820
only a digital service.

01:07:41.820 --> 01:07:44.940
This is about enabling
a slick digital service

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:47.940
for those patients who
want to access that.

01:07:47.940 --> 01:07:51.150
The upshot of that will
be we free up phone lines

01:07:51.150 --> 01:07:53.520
for people who would rather phone up

01:07:53.520 --> 01:07:55.620
in the traditional way
and talk to somebody

01:07:55.620 --> 01:07:57.120
about what the problem is,

01:07:57.120 --> 01:07:58.950
and obviously patients will still be able

01:07:58.950 --> 01:08:02.013
to walk into a reception
desk and make their request.

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:06.270
Alongside that, we also
recognise that once everybody

01:08:06.270 --> 01:08:09.930
can get through, we've
got to be able to make

01:08:09.930 --> 01:08:13.890
clinical capacity available
to meet that demand.

01:08:13.890 --> 01:08:16.890
And so we are building
capacity so that practises

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:18.270
can offer more appointments.

01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:23.270
We alluded earlier practise
in March this year offered 13%

01:08:23.490 --> 01:08:25.470
more appointments in
general practise, than in

01:08:25.470 --> 01:08:30.470
in the corresponding period
in 2019 before the pandemic.

01:08:31.620 --> 01:08:35.490
So despite increasing demand
and in spite pressures

01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:37.830
of on the workforce,

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:40.890
general practise is
stepping up and delivering

01:08:40.890 --> 01:08:43.203
more appointments than ever before.

01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:49.260
We are also, we're making
available this year

01:08:49.260 --> 01:08:53.010
an extra 385 million for
the additional roles staff.

01:08:53.010 --> 01:08:54.990
So that was, if you remember,

01:08:54.990 --> 01:08:57.780
this is the final year or the fifth year

01:08:57.780 --> 01:08:59.850
in the current contract arrangement

01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:02.610
of the additional roles scheme

01:09:02.610 --> 01:09:04.830
where practise and primary care networks

01:09:04.830 --> 01:09:07.590
are reimbursed to employ a wide range

01:09:07.590 --> 01:09:11.190
of clinical professionals
to increase the skill mix.

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:14.520
Really pleased that the
primary care workforce figures

01:09:14.520 --> 01:09:17.940
published today shows that we've employed

01:09:17.940 --> 01:09:22.290
an extra 29,000 direct patient care staff

01:09:22.290 --> 01:09:24.060
in general practise,

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:29.060
which means we've exceeded the
26,000 target a year early.

01:09:29.190 --> 01:09:30.657
So we're really pleased about that,

01:09:30.657 --> 01:09:32.940
and those staff are helping us deliver

01:09:32.940 --> 01:09:34.683
all of these extra appointments.

01:09:35.760 --> 01:09:37.020
Long-term workforce plan,

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:39.450
which we're all looking forward to.

01:09:39.450 --> 01:09:41.970
We'll give more information
about how we further expand

01:09:41.970 --> 01:09:43.740
GP specialty training,

01:09:43.740 --> 01:09:45.150
and we're working with the home office

01:09:45.150 --> 01:09:48.870
to expand availability of visas to allow

01:09:48.870 --> 01:09:51.600
newly qualified international graduate GPs

01:09:51.600 --> 01:09:54.240
to remain in the country for longer

01:09:54.240 --> 01:09:58.800
without having to go
through the processing

01:09:58.800 --> 01:10:00.903
and rush to extend visas.

01:10:01.770 --> 01:10:04.050
Pension reforms in the
budget are a big step

01:10:04.050 --> 01:10:06.090
towards encouraging experienced GPs

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:07.650
to remain in the workforce,

01:10:07.650 --> 01:10:10.590
but we're also creating
simple roots back to practise

01:10:10.590 --> 01:10:14.820
for experienced or recently retired GPs.

01:10:14.820 --> 01:10:17.130
And the government committed to put stuff

01:10:17.130 --> 01:10:18.660
in planning guidance,

01:10:18.660 --> 01:10:20.280
which commits local authorities

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:22.620
to considering primary
care service provision

01:10:22.620 --> 01:10:24.990
when they give planning permission

01:10:24.990 --> 01:10:27.960
for big housing developments.

01:10:27.960 --> 01:10:30.030
We've published alongside the plan

01:10:30.030 --> 01:10:32.730
the "Academy of Medical
Royal Colleges Report"

01:10:32.730 --> 01:10:35.850
that we commissioned at
the end of last year,

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:37.740
which talks about how
we improve the interface

01:10:37.740 --> 01:10:39.780
between primary and secondary care,

01:10:39.780 --> 01:10:42.900
how we avoid handoffs for patients,

01:10:42.900 --> 01:10:45.240
inappropriately, which
just increases the amount

01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:46.950
of bureaucracy in practise.

01:10:46.950 --> 01:10:51.810
And all of this is in response
to what GPs are telling us

01:10:51.810 --> 01:10:55.170
about things which increase
their workload significantly

01:10:55.170 --> 01:10:59.070
without any additional
clinical benefit to patients.

01:10:59.070 --> 01:11:02.460
And we've put an ask in
for Integrated Care Boards

01:11:02.460 --> 01:11:03.780
to report in public,

01:11:03.780 --> 01:11:04.710
and at their public boards

01:11:04.710 --> 01:11:06.720
about the measures they are taking

01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:09.300
to ensure that those
inappropriate handoffs

01:11:09.300 --> 01:11:11.643
and additional bureaucracy is tackled.

01:11:12.870 --> 01:11:14.610
Continuing to work with the government

01:11:14.610 --> 01:11:18.000
around their bureaucracy
busting concord out,

01:11:18.000 --> 01:11:22.350
so reducing excessive
asks for medical evidence.

01:11:22.350 --> 01:11:27.060
And we've responded to
the ask from practises

01:11:27.060 --> 01:11:29.820
who say that a lot of our processes

01:11:29.820 --> 01:11:34.800
are unnecessarily bureaucratic
with too many targets.

01:11:34.800 --> 01:11:37.380
And we streamlined the
Investment and Impact Fund

01:11:37.380 --> 01:11:42.090
down to just five indicators
from 36 last year.

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:45.720
And importantly, that
money is going to be paid,

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:48.990
or 70% of it up upfront
to primary care networks

01:11:48.990 --> 01:11:52.740
to enable them to spend
it on increasing capacity

01:11:52.740 --> 01:11:55.590
to address access rather than receiving it

01:11:55.590 --> 01:11:58.317
as an achievement payment
at the end of the year.

01:11:58.317 --> 01:11:59.640
And we're protecting

01:11:59.640 --> 01:12:02.670
some of the quality and
outcome framework indicators,

01:12:02.670 --> 01:12:04.380
so there's no risk to practise income.

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:06.900
And again, they can
therefore confidently commit

01:12:06.900 --> 01:12:08.400
to spending it upfront.

01:12:08.400 --> 01:12:10.200
And we've committed that
we will fully review

01:12:10.200 --> 01:12:12.600
the quality and outcome
framework for next year.

01:12:13.770 --> 01:12:16.050
As Amanda mentioned earlier,

01:12:16.050 --> 01:12:17.520
it's all very well writing a plan,

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:21.180
the challenge is in
getting it implemented,

01:12:21.180 --> 01:12:22.870
and we are developing a plan

01:12:24.270 --> 01:12:27.870
with regional teams
Integrated Care Boards,

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:31.290
and particularly with Tim's digital teams,

01:12:31.290 --> 01:12:35.610
and support from Jackie's commercial team.

01:12:35.610 --> 01:12:39.300
And once we publish the
long-term workforce plan,

01:12:39.300 --> 01:12:41.270
the stuff that we need to do

01:12:41.270 --> 01:12:43.650
to address the workforce issues in that.

01:12:43.650 --> 01:12:47.190
So we'll be bringing
things back to the Board

01:12:47.190 --> 01:12:51.000
over the next year or two years

01:12:51.000 --> 01:12:53.370
to report how we are
doing in delivering this.

01:12:53.370 --> 01:12:54.203
- So, Amanda, thank you.

01:12:54.203 --> 01:12:55.890
I'm going to open up
comments or questions.

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:59.353
I might get Amanda Pritchard also...

01:12:59.353 --> 01:13:00.720
Can I say Pritchard and Doyle,

01:13:00.720 --> 01:13:02.910
- Yeah.
- obviously with two Amandas.

01:13:02.910 --> 01:13:04.833
Sounds like a detective series.

01:13:07.830 --> 01:13:10.140
to comment in a moment, but I think

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:12.510
what you have just talked to us though,

01:13:12.510 --> 01:13:14.010
I think it's truly important,

01:13:14.010 --> 01:13:16.560
and it showed the benefits actually

01:13:16.560 --> 01:13:20.010
of one NHS England, you and your team.

01:13:20.010 --> 01:13:22.970
Thank you very much for a
really thoughtful production

01:13:22.970 --> 01:13:24.690
of a series of changes

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:27.000
that we can make working in partnership

01:13:27.000 --> 01:13:30.060
with the central state
and the relevant ministers

01:13:30.060 --> 01:13:32.010
in the Department of Health, very good,

01:13:32.010 --> 01:13:35.460
and with treasury and with
number 10 to produce this.

01:13:35.460 --> 01:13:38.250
And I think that was a
really good cooperation.

01:13:38.250 --> 01:13:41.880
Then I look at the elements

01:13:41.880 --> 01:13:44.367
within the plan, and even
that one of those last things

01:13:44.367 --> 01:13:46.350
you talked about, which was the,

01:13:46.350 --> 01:13:48.930
I'm essentially streamlining the IIF

01:13:48.930 --> 01:13:51.060
from 36 to five.

01:13:51.060 --> 01:13:52.380
And then actually it was then

01:13:52.380 --> 01:13:54.390
the additional thought process that says,

01:13:54.390 --> 01:13:56.181
and actually we're also going to shift

01:13:56.181 --> 01:13:57.847
from a payment of those,

01:13:57.847 --> 01:14:02.847
or for 70% of that, from an
as achieved basis, historic,

01:14:03.270 --> 01:14:05.970
to one that is paid essentially in advance

01:14:05.970 --> 01:14:07.890
to allow capacity spend.

01:14:07.890 --> 01:14:10.863
I mean it's really a very thoughtful plan,

01:14:12.338 --> 01:14:14.070
and again, we go back to it.

01:14:14.070 --> 01:14:16.650
And I, you know, I do
get the chance to meet

01:14:16.650 --> 01:14:19.350
and go around primary care practises,

01:14:19.350 --> 01:14:21.210
and I do hear about the bureaucracy,

01:14:21.210 --> 01:14:22.650
and the amount of reporting how to do,

01:14:22.650 --> 01:14:25.500
and it's like 20% of what we
ask primary care practises,

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:27.753
or GPs to do with a non-medical,

01:14:29.070 --> 01:14:31.560
either voucher saves or other sign-offs

01:14:31.560 --> 01:14:34.020
for society overall.

01:14:34.020 --> 01:14:36.420
We know we said earlier, 1.1
million appointments a day,

01:14:36.420 --> 01:14:40.350
almost half of those
appointments are on the same day

01:14:40.350 --> 01:14:43.107
that the actual contact,
the phone call is made,

01:14:43.107 --> 01:14:46.470
and two-thirds of them are face-to-face.

01:14:46.470 --> 01:14:47.940
And then also just to reflect,

01:14:47.940 --> 01:14:51.060
you know, we've seen
you said a 13% increase?

01:14:51.060 --> 01:14:52.650
So the demand levels are up.
- Yeah.

01:14:52.650 --> 01:14:55.020
- So 13% increase in the
number of appointments

01:14:55.020 --> 01:14:58.740
being offered, attempting
to meet the demand,

01:14:58.740 --> 01:15:00.690
the rising demand of the population.

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:03.300
It is a really important
part of our system.

01:15:03.300 --> 01:15:06.720
You know, it's an insurance
gateway for the NHS,

01:15:06.720 --> 01:15:10.443
and it's an assurance
giver to the communities

01:15:10.443 --> 01:15:14.460
that the primary care businesses serve.

01:15:14.460 --> 01:15:16.260
And I often think, actually,
you've heard me say it,

01:15:16.260 --> 01:15:19.320
it sounds a bit rhapsodic or poetic.

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:21.540
You know, it's a, you are a GP,

01:15:21.540 --> 01:15:24.960
it's an amazing vocation
and they work hugely hard

01:15:24.960 --> 01:15:26.700
and they're absolutely our frontline

01:15:26.700 --> 01:15:29.610
dealing with what 93% of
the population's engagement

01:15:29.610 --> 01:15:32.940
