01:27:37.080 --> 01:27:39.960
So why is this such an important topic?

01:27:39.960 --> 01:27:44.010
Well, actually several people, I've noted,

01:27:44.010 --> 01:27:45.870
during the course of just this meeting,

01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:49.350
several people have mentioned
the demographic wave,

01:27:49.350 --> 01:27:51.000
Amanda Doyle did it so beautifully,

01:27:51.000 --> 01:27:52.350
I'm not going to repeat it,

01:27:52.350 --> 01:27:56.700
but the increasing numbers
of the older population

01:27:56.700 --> 01:28:01.290
of which I am a proud member
with multimorbid conditions.

01:28:01.290 --> 01:28:05.790
And that is one element

01:28:05.790 --> 01:28:10.710
of the pressure on the NHS
to deliver more services.

01:28:10.710 --> 01:28:15.420
But the other major element
is in fact the success

01:28:15.420 --> 01:28:17.490
of the biologic sciences,

01:28:17.490 --> 01:28:19.320
and the data sciences

01:28:19.320 --> 01:28:22.860
to provide more diagnostics and therapies.

01:28:22.860 --> 01:28:26.640
And so what we have is greater demand,

01:28:26.640 --> 01:28:28.950
but also, and this is a great thing.

01:28:28.950 --> 01:28:33.120
It's great news that we have
more tools to reduce suffering,

01:28:33.120 --> 01:28:37.080
but we are adding those
tools onto a chassis,

01:28:37.080 --> 01:28:39.960
a delivery chassis that
was essentially designed

01:28:39.960 --> 01:28:43.950
in the 1950s, and really hasn't been

01:28:43.950 --> 01:28:46.410
very thoroughly updated since then.

01:28:46.410 --> 01:28:50.430
And that to me, that's
how I would articulate

01:28:50.430 --> 01:28:53.580
the challenge of the pressures

01:28:53.580 --> 01:28:58.443
that we see across primary
care, ambulance service, A&amp;E,

01:29:00.216 --> 01:29:02.190
the acute hospitals,

01:29:02.190 --> 01:29:04.050
post-acute hospitals, elective,

01:29:04.050 --> 01:29:07.110
all of those services are services

01:29:07.110 --> 01:29:10.770
to deliver these wonderful new tools

01:29:10.770 --> 01:29:14.100
that we have to an increasing size

01:29:14.100 --> 01:29:17.913
of population that has treatable illness.

01:29:19.224 --> 01:29:24.224
So, that is, as Julian well knows,

01:29:24.510 --> 01:29:26.463
that is placing financial pressures,

01:29:27.330 --> 01:29:30.210
and as Navina knows, that is placing

01:29:30.210 --> 01:29:32.310
incredible workforce pressures.

01:29:32.310 --> 01:29:34.890
So how are we going to get out of this?

01:29:34.890 --> 01:29:38.310
And so, technology is part of the problem.

01:29:38.310 --> 01:29:40.650
I do think, and this is
just echoing what Laura said

01:29:40.650 --> 01:29:41.940
a minute ago,

01:29:41.940 --> 01:29:45.450
technology is a major part
of the solution, okay?

01:29:45.450 --> 01:29:50.450
So we can use technology to
do three critical things.

01:29:51.150 --> 01:29:53.250
We must reduce the friction,

01:29:53.250 --> 01:29:56.250
the transactional friction that occurs

01:29:56.250 --> 01:29:59.010
when people in the healthcare service

01:29:59.010 --> 01:30:02.520
and people seeking those
services experience.

01:30:02.520 --> 01:30:04.950
And I have experienced that
transactional friction,

01:30:04.950 --> 01:30:08.373
any clinician experiences
that transactional friction.

01:30:09.210 --> 01:30:13.110
Actually Steve said it
well just a minute ago,

01:30:13.110 --> 01:30:15.630
decreasing the bureaucracy,
- Yeah.

01:30:15.630 --> 01:30:18.360
- associated with those transactions,

01:30:18.360 --> 01:30:21.420
and I'll speak to some
specific examples in a second.

01:30:21.420 --> 01:30:23.190
The second thing is we have to reduce

01:30:23.190 --> 01:30:26.670
the number of steps in pathways.

01:30:26.670 --> 01:30:30.630
We want to preference technology

01:30:30.630 --> 01:30:34.830
that removes entire steps in pathways.

01:30:34.830 --> 01:30:37.500
Again, there's some great
examples, I'll get to in a moment.

01:30:37.500 --> 01:30:41.580
And then we also want to make sure that,

01:30:41.580 --> 01:30:43.410
or to, as much as possible,

01:30:43.410 --> 01:30:46.830
the technology that we
are bringing in to replace

01:30:46.830 --> 01:30:51.360
existing ways of working
is cash releasing.

01:30:51.360 --> 01:30:52.530
In other words,

01:30:52.530 --> 01:30:56.040
year over year budgets
are actually going down,

01:30:56.040 --> 01:30:59.940
as a result of the implementation
of this technology.

01:30:59.940 --> 01:31:01.500
Now this is a pretty high bar

01:31:01.500 --> 01:31:02.333
that I've just set.
- Yeah.

01:31:02.333 --> 01:31:03.540
- But actually,

01:31:03.540 --> 01:31:06.840
it's doable and we have great examples

01:31:06.840 --> 01:31:09.870
and increasing examples of this happening.

01:31:09.870 --> 01:31:12.570
So what is our current strategy?

01:31:12.570 --> 01:31:17.570
So we think about the set of
tools in three categories,

01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:20.880
digitise, connect, and transform, right?

01:31:20.880 --> 01:31:24.843
So digitises, you can't do
the transformational work,

01:31:26.610 --> 01:31:29.010
if your services aren't
digitised to begin with.

01:31:29.010 --> 01:31:32.100
We still have work to do
and it's really important

01:31:32.100 --> 01:31:35.070
that we do that work
of digitising services.

01:31:35.070 --> 01:31:37.410
So, we often talk

01:31:37.410 --> 01:31:40.050
about electronic patient records, EPRs.

01:31:40.050 --> 01:31:43.980
We're currently at 88%
of healthcare services

01:31:43.980 --> 01:31:46.717
district general hospitals,
the acute hospitals,

01:31:46.717 --> 01:31:50.013
- Yeah.
- have an enterprise EPR.

01:31:52.380 --> 01:31:57.380
We are on track to be over
90% come this December,

01:31:57.390 --> 01:32:00.570
and really we have to finish that process.

01:32:00.570 --> 01:32:03.210
We also have to digitise social care.

01:32:03.210 --> 01:32:07.200
I will say the speed
with which social care

01:32:07.200 --> 01:32:10.260
is becoming digitised is extraordinary.

01:32:10.260 --> 01:32:13.290
So just a couple years
ago it was below 20%.

01:32:13.290 --> 01:32:15.180
It's now over 40%,

01:32:15.180 --> 01:32:18.219
and the speed with which
it's being taken up

01:32:18.219 --> 01:32:19.920
is actually quite dramatic.

01:32:19.920 --> 01:32:21.630
So, I'm very pleased

01:32:21.630 --> 01:32:23.640
with the progress we've seen there.

01:32:23.640 --> 01:32:27.180
And then, I'll just
mention one other system

01:32:27.180 --> 01:32:28.260
in the digitised front,

01:32:28.260 --> 01:32:31.080
which is electronic
bed management systems.

01:32:31.080 --> 01:32:34.500
So we talked, Sarah Jane
gave us a terrific discussion

01:32:34.500 --> 01:32:36.870
of length of stay.

01:32:36.870 --> 01:32:39.390
So length of stay is
very difficult to manage.

01:32:39.390 --> 01:32:41.643
It's about clinicians managing risk,

01:32:42.480 --> 01:32:44.820
nurses and doctors
managing risk in hospitals.

01:32:44.820 --> 01:32:46.710
But with the right tools,

01:32:46.710 --> 01:32:49.530
they can do a better job of managing risk.

01:32:49.530 --> 01:32:53.160
So we have data that shows that EPRs

01:32:53.160 --> 01:32:57.510
produce at least 7% increase efficiencies

01:32:57.510 --> 01:33:00.960
in outcomes like length of stay, 7%.

01:33:00.960 --> 01:33:02.783
That is a big change.
- Yeah.

01:33:02.783 --> 01:33:04.860
- That is a significant change,

01:33:04.860 --> 01:33:09.390
but bed management systems
like the one at Maidstone

01:33:09.390 --> 01:33:12.810
actually can produce even bigger benefits

01:33:12.810 --> 01:33:15.870
on making sure that we
are maximising the use

01:33:15.870 --> 01:33:17.940
of our most expensive assets,

01:33:17.940 --> 01:33:20.613
like theatres and hospital beds,

01:33:21.503 --> 01:33:24.810
and so it is through the
use of these digital tools

01:33:24.810 --> 01:33:27.090
that that becomes enabled.

01:33:27.090 --> 01:33:30.899
We have a terrific programme
that we're working on

01:33:30.899 --> 01:33:32.820
with the Department of Health

01:33:32.820 --> 01:33:36.030
around electronic bed management systems,

01:33:36.030 --> 01:33:39.120
and seeing really, again, rapid uptake.

01:33:39.120 --> 01:33:42.060
It's sort of pushing against an open door.

01:33:42.060 --> 01:33:43.800
Let me say a word about connect.

01:33:43.800 --> 01:33:48.630
So connecting our information services.

01:33:48.630 --> 01:33:51.780
So all of us want and should expect

01:33:51.780 --> 01:33:55.680
that if you visit any
clinician in this country,

01:33:55.680 --> 01:33:58.530
whether they're in your
home region or not,

01:33:58.530 --> 01:34:00.900
that that clinician has
the information they need

01:34:00.900 --> 01:34:02.790
to take optimal care of you.

01:34:02.790 --> 01:34:07.790
So recently now all ICSs
have shared care records.

01:34:08.490 --> 01:34:13.050
So all clinicians in
those ICSs have access

01:34:13.050 --> 01:34:17.613
to one of 14 sub-regional
shared care records.

01:34:18.540 --> 01:34:20.550
We've now had this for about a year,

01:34:20.550 --> 01:34:22.290
but our work is,

01:34:22.290 --> 01:34:23.760
that's only part of the journey, right?

01:34:23.760 --> 01:34:27.060
We now have the next
task of making sure that,

01:34:27.060 --> 01:34:30.120
and we have plans to
extend this nationally.

01:34:30.120 --> 01:34:32.400
How are we going to make the standards,

01:34:32.400 --> 01:34:34.050
the data standards and interoperability

01:34:34.050 --> 01:34:36.480
across those 14 shared care records,

01:34:36.480 --> 01:34:39.090
so that we do achieve that national goal?

01:34:39.090 --> 01:34:41.670
That is within our reach
and looking forward

01:34:41.670 --> 01:34:44.400
to the work ahead on that.

01:34:44.400 --> 01:34:46.230
The next one is,

01:34:46.230 --> 01:34:49.500
a lot of people don't know currently

01:34:49.500 --> 01:34:53.400
a lot of data in the NHS is and has been

01:34:53.400 --> 01:34:56.010
for a long time managed
at the national level,

01:34:56.010 --> 01:34:58.980
so the definitive source of information.

01:34:58.980 --> 01:35:02.110
So, earlier, Richard, you mentioned

01:35:03.582 --> 01:35:07.290
the number of visits per day in the NHS.

01:35:07.290 --> 01:35:12.290
So every time a patient
registers in any NHS site,

01:35:14.280 --> 01:35:16.480
that site is pinging

01:35:17.610 --> 01:35:20.190
the national patient demographic service.

01:35:20.190 --> 01:35:22.710
So over a million times a day,

01:35:22.710 --> 01:35:27.600
this service is documenting the
fact that the correct person

01:35:27.600 --> 01:35:31.770
is sitting and that then
identifies the other information

01:35:31.770 --> 01:35:34.797
that's available on that through
something called the Spine,

01:35:34.797 --> 01:35:36.270
the NHS Spine.

01:35:36.270 --> 01:35:40.500
The NHS Spine is halfway
through a migration to the cloud

01:35:40.500 --> 01:35:42.780
to modernise its services.

01:35:42.780 --> 01:35:44.688
This has been so far,

01:35:44.688 --> 01:35:45.720
knock on wood,

01:35:45.720 --> 01:35:49.080
a flawless process because
of the extraordinary people

01:35:49.080 --> 01:35:51.150
who have been committed to making sure

01:35:51.150 --> 01:35:55.110
this technology is the
very best that it can be.

01:35:55.110 --> 01:35:59.430
And then of course there's
the federated data platform.

01:35:59.430 --> 01:36:04.080
So the goal here is
that every ICS will have

01:36:04.080 --> 01:36:07.830
its own platform for local innovations

01:36:07.830 --> 01:36:11.970
to build tools, like Chelsea
and Westminster built,

01:36:11.970 --> 01:36:15.630
to reduce their waiting
list by maximising the use

01:36:15.630 --> 01:36:18.840
of the data from all
their different systems

01:36:18.840 --> 01:36:21.660
coming to a very specific
programme that they built

01:36:21.660 --> 01:36:25.380
that sits on top of a
federated data platform.

01:36:25.380 --> 01:36:29.220
That federated data
platform can then be shared

01:36:29.220 --> 01:36:33.330
to any organisation within the country

01:36:33.330 --> 01:36:37.800
because of the very federated
nature of the data platform.

01:36:37.800 --> 01:36:39.780
And so we are well through what,

01:36:39.780 --> 01:36:42.510
we're well into the procurement process

01:36:42.510 --> 01:36:44.640
for the federated data platform.

01:36:44.640 --> 01:36:47.970
I'm going to finish up
with the transform part.

01:36:47.970 --> 01:36:52.680
So that's all table stakes
for really what we need to do

01:36:52.680 --> 01:36:55.287
on the transformation end,

01:36:55.287 --> 01:36:58.980
and I think of transformation
as in two buckets.

01:36:58.980 --> 01:37:03.980
One is a pathway specific
bucket of technology,

01:37:04.140 --> 01:37:08.520
and then there's technology
that implicates everything

01:37:08.520 --> 01:37:10.050
throughout the health system.

01:37:10.050 --> 01:37:12.840
So on the pathway specific,

01:37:12.840 --> 01:37:16.710
again, what we want is lower
transactional friction,

01:37:16.710 --> 01:37:20.070
remove steps from the
pathway and cash releasing.

01:37:20.070 --> 01:37:23.970
So let me just cite a couple
of really exciting things

01:37:23.970 --> 01:37:25.530
that are going on.

01:37:25.530 --> 01:37:29.332
So, first of all, and
I'm not going to mention

01:37:29.332 --> 01:37:34.332
any commercial names here,
but I just want to say there,

01:37:34.410 --> 01:37:39.410
as I travel throughout
the NHS, every single ICS,

01:37:39.750 --> 01:37:41.280
every single primary care practise,

01:37:41.280 --> 01:37:44.910
every single trust is doing
amazing innovation work

01:37:44.910 --> 01:37:46.470
in this pathway space.

01:37:46.470 --> 01:37:48.360
They have some entrepreneur,

01:37:48.360 --> 01:37:53.160
so a cardiologist, a nurse
oncologist who has said,

01:37:53.160 --> 01:37:55.650
has taken on the challenge
of, you know what,

01:37:55.650 --> 01:37:56.940
we can make this better.

01:37:56.940 --> 01:38:00.060
And they're using technology
to solve a problem

01:38:00.060 --> 01:38:01.680
right in front of them.

01:38:01.680 --> 01:38:03.447
So in the, I'll,

01:38:03.447 --> 01:38:07.350
and it's, I hesitant to
give specific examples

01:38:07.350 --> 01:38:09.390
because there's so many others,

01:38:09.390 --> 01:38:11.100
but, just to illustrate.

01:38:11.100 --> 01:38:14.940
So there is new technology
that is essentially headphones

01:38:14.940 --> 01:38:18.930
that you put on that both
visualise the tympanic membrane

01:38:18.930 --> 01:38:23.160
and conduct an audiology
exam, so a hearing test.

01:38:23.160 --> 01:38:26.760
So you are both doing the
visual work, which is,

01:38:26.760 --> 01:38:28.530
by the way, that's one specialty,

01:38:28.530 --> 01:38:30.600
and you're doing the audiology work,

01:38:30.600 --> 01:38:33.390
that's a separate allied
health profession.

01:38:33.390 --> 01:38:36.810
In a single step, you are doing both.

01:38:36.810 --> 01:38:40.620
And the patients at UCLH
who've used this system

01:38:40.620 --> 01:38:44.193
implemented in a few GP practises,

01:38:45.420 --> 01:38:49.410
65% of them have avoided
any further follow-up.

01:38:49.410 --> 01:38:54.180
They have the problem solved
as a result of this process.

01:38:54.180 --> 01:38:58.440
Like that is one example
of removing multiple steps

01:38:58.440 --> 01:39:03.440
and decreasing friction in the
transaction that occurs with,

01:39:03.720 --> 01:39:06.660
I have pain in my ear, how
can I solve that problem?

01:39:06.660 --> 01:39:09.360
Well, you can go to a pharmacy,

01:39:09.360 --> 01:39:12.600
put on headphones and have
your problem diagnosed

01:39:12.600 --> 01:39:15.780
because the pictures
are sent at a distance

01:39:15.780 --> 01:39:20.070
to an ENT surgeon who
can examine the pictures

01:39:20.070 --> 01:39:22.230
and make a diagnosis.

01:39:22.230 --> 01:39:24.900
That is the kind of reduction in friction

01:39:24.900 --> 01:39:26.970
and reduction in pathway steps

01:39:26.970 --> 01:39:30.120
that is transformational.

01:39:30.120 --> 01:39:34.590
Very quickly, so the same thing
is occurring in dermatology.

01:39:34.590 --> 01:39:36.750
So, dermatology, it's one

01:39:36.750 --> 01:39:40.440
of our longest secondary care waiting is,

01:39:40.440 --> 01:39:42.660
the highest numbers are in dermatology.

01:39:42.660 --> 01:39:46.350
Well, there are practises
in Birmingham that,

01:39:46.350 --> 01:39:48.630
and elsewhere in the country actually,

01:39:48.630 --> 01:39:51.210
Chelsea and Westminster
there's a number of them

01:39:51.210 --> 01:39:53.610
that have implemented software

01:39:53.610 --> 01:39:57.120
where you come in and
instead of seeing the doctor,

01:39:57.120 --> 01:39:59.820
you see a medical photographer.

01:39:59.820 --> 01:40:02.700
It takes a couple days to
train a medical photographer,

01:40:02.700 --> 01:40:04.980
and they take some
pictures of your lesion,

01:40:04.980 --> 01:40:06.690
run it through software.

01:40:06.690 --> 01:40:08.790
And that software doesn't,

01:40:08.790 --> 01:40:11.910
by the way make any decisions about care.

01:40:11.910 --> 01:40:14.430
It's what's called decision support.

01:40:14.430 --> 01:40:17.880
What it does is it
offers up to a clinician

01:40:17.880 --> 01:40:20.010
who's able to very rapidly...

01:40:20.010 --> 01:40:24.270
So there is a physician looking
at all of these lesions,

01:40:24.270 --> 01:40:27.580
but the software is helping them triage

01:40:31.901 --> 01:40:33.990
the pathology that they're seeing.

01:40:33.990 --> 01:40:38.880
And in those cases, more
than a third of patients

01:40:38.880 --> 01:40:40.290
are just given reassurance.

01:40:40.290 --> 01:40:44.340
Like that is not a problem,
is not at all a concern.

01:40:44.340 --> 01:40:47.490
That's more than a third of
patients waiting on a wait list

01:40:47.490 --> 01:40:49.680
to see a dermatologist are given

01:40:49.680 --> 01:40:52.137
the reassurance they need that is...

01:40:52.137 --> 01:40:54.120
And this technology, by the way,

01:40:54.120 --> 01:40:57.030
is highly validated to be highly sensitive

01:40:57.030 --> 01:40:58.800
and highly specific.

01:40:58.800 --> 01:41:00.510
We're doing, I won't go long,

01:41:00.510 --> 01:41:04.380
we're doing the same
thing with cardiology.

01:41:04.380 --> 01:41:06.300
One of the longest waiting
lists for imaging tests

01:41:06.300 --> 01:41:08.100
is for cardiac echoes.

01:41:08.100 --> 01:41:11.744
There is technology
now that allows anyone,

01:41:11.744 --> 01:41:13.680
- any trained clinician

01:41:13.680 --> 01:41:18.420
to examine the heart
without a separate process.

01:41:18.420 --> 01:41:21.570
Again, reducing steps in the pathway

01:41:21.570 --> 01:41:25.170
for understanding what
the ejection fraction,

01:41:25.170 --> 01:41:29.790
what the heart muscle is
operating, how the valves are.

01:41:29.790 --> 01:41:32.013
That is a really big,

01:41:33.512 --> 01:41:34.650
it's a narrow pathway,

01:41:34.650 --> 01:41:37.320
but it's a really big
change in that pathway.

01:41:37.320 --> 01:41:40.020
The same thing is true for chest X-rays.

01:41:40.020 --> 01:41:43.620
So the most common imaging
test obtained in the NHS

01:41:43.620 --> 01:41:46.890
is a chest X-ray, 600,000 of them.

01:41:46.890 --> 01:41:50.580
So, there are now AI
tools that can, again,

01:41:50.580 --> 01:41:54.450
they don't make a diagnosis
but they can support

01:41:54.450 --> 01:41:56.790
a diagnosis by a radiologist.

01:41:56.790 --> 01:41:58.890
And we are actively rolling out

01:41:58.890 --> 01:42:03.330
the AI tools for pre-reading chest X-rays,

01:42:03.330 --> 01:42:06.450
same is also true for mammograms.

01:42:06.450 --> 01:42:09.120
And then the last one I'll just cite is,

01:42:09.120 --> 01:42:12.060
that's a pathway specific is with stroke.

01:42:12.060 --> 01:42:15.270
So, as I'm sure all of
the clinicians will know,

01:42:15.270 --> 01:42:19.530
but most people know time
is essential in a stroke

01:42:19.530 --> 01:42:22.980
because every additional minute

01:42:22.980 --> 01:42:25.260
that someone waits for
a definitive therapy,

01:42:25.260 --> 01:42:28.650
when they're having a stroke has the risk

01:42:28.650 --> 01:42:33.090
of making the symptoms
worse or lasting longer,

01:42:33.090 --> 01:42:35.010
so, minutes count.

01:42:35.010 --> 01:42:38.940
There is AI technology that reads CT scans

01:42:38.940 --> 01:42:42.420
for anyone in any
district general hospital,

01:42:42.420 --> 01:42:44.790
away from a major stroke centre,

01:42:44.790 --> 01:42:49.320
that allows a diagnosis
to be made and therapy

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:50.550
to be delivered.

01:42:50.550 --> 01:42:53.250
It has reduced by 60 minutes,

01:42:53.250 --> 01:42:54.870
where this has been implemented,

01:42:54.870 --> 01:42:57.840
it is reduced by 60 minutes
the time to therapy.

01:42:57.840 --> 01:43:01.233
60 minutes in a stroke
patient is a really big deal.

01:43:03.120 --> 01:43:05.460
So those are the pathway specific,

01:43:05.460 --> 01:43:10.460
and those are, again,
there's just a smattering.

01:43:10.560 --> 01:43:12.900
But I want to say just conclude

01:43:12.900 --> 01:43:16.890
by some of the ubiquitous
transformational things.

01:43:16.890 --> 01:43:19.500
So the first one I want to just mention,

01:43:19.500 --> 01:43:21.180
and it's been mentioned
a couple times already,

01:43:21.180 --> 01:43:23.460
it's very important for
the primary care recovery,

01:43:23.460 --> 01:43:24.750
is the app.

01:43:24.750 --> 01:43:28.140
And just making sure
that people understand

01:43:28.140 --> 01:43:32.430
how many transactions that occur for,

01:43:32.430 --> 01:43:34.530
when you make a follow up appointment

01:43:34.530 --> 01:43:36.390
with a physician's office,

01:43:36.390 --> 01:43:40.300
on average it takes 2.5 phone calls

01:43:41.454 --> 01:43:44.460
to make one visit, okay?

01:43:44.460 --> 01:43:47.250
And actually that's a
completed phone call.

01:43:47.250 --> 01:43:50.250
They're all of the, you know, calling out

01:43:50.250 --> 01:43:52.290
to get the appointment and no one answers,

01:43:52.290 --> 01:43:53.490
or whatever it is.

01:43:53.490 --> 01:43:55.560
So, all of that friction,

01:43:55.560 --> 01:43:58.110
it turns out if you
can make an appointment

01:43:58.110 --> 01:44:00.930
for a follow-up visit on your phone,

01:44:00.930 --> 01:44:03.780
not only does it dramatically
reduce the friction

01:44:03.780 --> 01:44:07.500
in the system and free up
time for front office staff,

01:44:07.500 --> 01:44:11.700
but also when patients make
their own appointments,

01:44:11.700 --> 01:44:14.670
the chance of them actually
attending that appointment

01:44:14.670 --> 01:44:16.320
- Yeah.
- goes up dramatically.

01:44:16.320 --> 01:44:18.933
And so it makes the whole
system more efficient.

01:44:20.040 --> 01:44:23.760
The number of people who are
using the app functionality

01:44:23.760 --> 01:44:25.830
to make appointments is
going up dramatically.

01:44:25.830 --> 01:44:30.060
And we are actively
pushing patient portals

01:44:30.060 --> 01:44:32.820
out to all of our secondary
care institutions,

01:44:32.820 --> 01:44:35.970
so that we want to make
sure this functionality

01:44:35.970 --> 01:44:39.633
is available ubiquitously for citizens.

01:44:40.980 --> 01:44:44.370
Ambient documentation, so
let me just say a word about,

01:44:44.370 --> 01:44:46.680
so clinicians, nurses and doctors

01:44:46.680 --> 01:44:51.030
spend between 20 and
30% of their work time

01:44:51.030 --> 01:44:52.833
documenting their work.

01:44:54.240 --> 01:44:58.260
Think or imagine if they
didn't have to spend time

01:44:58.260 --> 01:44:59.700
documenting their work.

01:44:59.700 --> 01:45:03.600
The technology to make
that happen exists today.

01:45:03.600 --> 01:45:05.850
It's called ambient documentation.

01:45:05.850 --> 01:45:09.990
It will be a while before it's
fully scaled in healthcare,

01:45:09.990 --> 01:45:12.030
but it is coming very quickly.

01:45:12.030 --> 01:45:16.230
Anyone who's used the AI
tools that are now available

01:45:16.230 --> 01:45:19.740
on the web knows this
is coming very quickly.

01:45:19.740 --> 01:45:22.770
I've seen it demonstrated. I've used it.

01:45:22.770 --> 01:45:27.770
And it will be a major step
change in productivity.

01:45:28.050 --> 01:45:29.670
I just want to assure everyone,

01:45:29.670 --> 01:45:32.010
the NHS is working
really hard to make sure

01:45:32.010 --> 01:45:34.290
we're at the front of the line for-

01:45:34.290 --> 01:45:35.190
- [Richard] How does it operate?

01:45:35.190 --> 01:45:36.240
How does it work?

01:45:36.240 --> 01:45:38.170
- So it's basically-
- You're the clinician.

01:45:38.170 --> 01:45:41.400
- Yeah, you're a clinician and
you're talking to a patient,

01:45:41.400 --> 01:45:45.120
whether you're talking
virtually or in the same room,

01:45:45.120 --> 01:45:47.190
and you have a microphone in the room.

01:45:47.190 --> 01:45:48.990
It could be your phone in fact.

01:45:48.990 --> 01:45:52.770
And the phone makes, it's
called voice to text, right?

01:45:52.770 --> 01:45:53.760
Everyone knows how to do that.

01:45:53.760 --> 01:45:56.520
You speak to your phone and
it just creates the text.

01:45:56.520 --> 01:45:57.630
So voice to text is

01:45:57.630 --> 01:45:59.220
sort of old technology.
- Yeah.

01:45:59.220 --> 01:46:00.450
- Everyone does that.

01:46:00.450 --> 01:46:04.050
The tricky thing here now is that with AI

01:46:04.050 --> 01:46:08.100
you can actually take a
transcript of a conversation

01:46:08.100 --> 01:46:13.100
and completely reformulate
it into a clinical note

01:46:13.170 --> 01:46:15.810
that is precise.

01:46:15.810 --> 01:46:18.030
And then all the clinician has to do,

01:46:18.030 --> 01:46:19.500
instead of generating the note,

01:46:19.500 --> 01:46:22.170
the clinician just has
to examine the note.

01:46:22.170 --> 01:46:25.800
Make a few edits if they're
necessary, and they're done.

01:46:25.800 --> 01:46:27.900
They're done documenting.

01:46:27.900 --> 01:46:32.010
That is more than a 90% improvement

01:46:32.010 --> 01:46:37.010
in the bureaucracy associated
with documentation.

01:46:37.200 --> 01:46:39.720
That is what I would say as a,

01:46:39.720 --> 01:46:42.060
that's one of these things
where every clinician

01:46:42.060 --> 01:46:43.140
has to document.

01:46:43.140 --> 01:46:45.510
So this doesn't affect one pathway,

01:46:45.510 --> 01:46:47.760
this affects all of healthcare,

01:46:47.760 --> 01:46:50.310
and that is coming and the NHS is working.

01:46:50.310 --> 01:46:53.730
We're actively working with
suppliers of this technology

01:46:53.730 --> 01:46:57.633
to see how we're going to
incorporate this into the NHS.

01:46:59.184 --> 01:47:02.010
And then I wanted to finish

01:47:02.010 --> 01:47:04.080
with digital health therapeutics.

01:47:04.080 --> 01:47:07.380
So this is another real game changer.

01:47:07.380 --> 01:47:12.380
So is it possible to deliver
therapeutic interventions

01:47:12.660 --> 01:47:15.630
without people involved at all?

01:47:15.630 --> 01:47:17.980
Now this is where we're
getting out at the edge

01:47:19.441 --> 01:47:21.030
of what is possible.

01:47:21.030 --> 01:47:23.100
But for example,

01:47:23.100 --> 01:47:26.190
in musculoskeletal care
and mental healthcare,

01:47:26.190 --> 01:47:30.423
and I'm talking about
relatively mild mental illness.

01:47:32.160 --> 01:47:34.980
There are now small numbers

01:47:34.980 --> 01:47:39.060
of relatively small randomised
trials that show, yes,

01:47:39.060 --> 01:47:44.060
you can in fact improve
patient outcomes safely

01:47:44.100 --> 01:47:48.420
by giving them access to
digital health therapeutics.

01:47:48.420 --> 01:47:53.163
And in the chancellor's March
announcement on the budget,

01:47:54.660 --> 01:47:56.730
funding was announced to actually do

01:47:56.730 --> 01:47:58.440
a big experiment,

01:47:58.440 --> 01:48:01.950
and we are going to try implementing

01:48:01.950 --> 01:48:04.260
digital health therapeutics
in mental health

01:48:04.260 --> 01:48:06.393
and musculoskeletal disease,

01:48:07.230 --> 01:48:08.790
across the population.

01:48:08.790 --> 01:48:11.793
So, this is big and ambitious work,

01:48:13.082 --> 01:48:16.350
and, again, potentially, well,
I shouldn't say potentially,

01:48:16.350 --> 01:48:18.870
it will be transformative.
- Good.

